INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3800,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11879301','Transitioning from pediatric to adult-oriented healthcare in rheumatology: the pediatric side of the coin.','Batu ED, Balik Z, Sener S, Aliyev E, Bayindir Y, Cam V, Kasap Cuceoglu M, Kaya Akca U, Kilic L, Basaran O, Bilginer Y, Apras Bilgen S, Ozen S.','2025','ayas ayas morisky mmas ayas traqs mmas ayas ayas ayas ayas hacettepe hacettepe ayas ayas hacettepe ayas ayas ayas morisky mmas mmas mmas mmas traqs traqs traqs traqs traqs traqs traqs traqs mctd mmas mmas mmas mmas unsuccessfully mmas morisky traqs hospitalize hospitalize unsuccessfully ayas hazel pelt unsuccessfully unsuccessfully ayas ayas lotstein ayas mmas ayas keae254_supplementary_data keae254_supplementary_data ozan uysal yesim ezgi deniz batu hacettepe zeynep balik hacettepe seher sener hacettepe aliyev hacettepe yagmur bayindir hacettepe veysel hacettepe muserref kasap cuceoglu hacettepe ummusen akca hacettepe levent kilic hacettepe ozge basaran hacettepe yelda bilginer hacettepe sule apras bilgen hacettepe seza ozen hacettepe coyne heery rwm garell hodgman miyamae bando sonmez aktay ayaz sozeri southwood kiziler yildiz fidanci questionaire sawicki morisky andemariam owarish sonmez koc karadag aktay ayaz lazaroff meara tompkins ardoin chapados aramideh lamore arvanitis sawicki tumiel berhalter lazarevic dordevic novakovic zecevic susic anelli len terreri reiff kittivisuit lerkvaleekul soponkanaporn ngamjanyaporn vilaiyuk oberoi sobota e29417 cleverley allemang brennenstuhl gaalen gijn pieterson ng43 hazel campillo pelt dolhain kruize lotstein seid e1062 mcbee livingood keae254_supplementary_data keae254_supplementary_data','Abstract Objectives The transition of adolescents and young adults (AYAs) from pediatric to adult-oriented healthcare may be affected by many factors, including the personal and cultural settings. We aimed to analyse the transition readiness and the factors affecting the transition success in rheumatology. Methods Patients older than 12 years were included in this prospective study. All filled out the Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ) 5.0. AYAs were phone-interviewed after their transfer to adult-oriented healthcare. Drug adherence was evaluated with 4-item Morisky Medication Adherence Scale (MMAS-4). AYAs rated their transitional care experience with visual analogue scale (VAS 0–10; 0, the worst; 10, the best). Results A total of 504 TRAQs were filled out by 406 patients (F/M = 1.5). The total TRAQ score was positively correlated with age and higher in the forms filled out by girls than boys (4.2 vs 4.0, respectively; P = 0.005). The transition was successful for 78 (83.9%) out of 93 patients transferred to adult-oriented healthcare. The VAS for the transition process was lower and the post-transfer MMAS-4 score was worse (8 vs 9, P = 0.030 and 3 vs 4, P = 0.020, respectively) in patients whose transition was not successful when compared with those that successfully transitioned. The best-performing TRAQ cut-off value was >4.0 for predicting transfer readiness in rheumatology. Conclusion A TRAQ score of >4 could be used while deciding about the transfer readiness of AYAs in rheumatology. Improving the AYAs’ experience of the transition process and closely monitoring medication adherence during transition are essential for a successful transition. Keywords: pediatric rheumatology, transitional care, TRAQ Graphical Abstract Open in a new tab Rheumatology key messages. The best-performing Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ) score was >4.0 for predicting transfer readiness in rheumatology. The medication adherence became worse, especially for patients whose transition was not successful. Monitoring medication adherence and providing a good transition experience are critical for the continuity of healthcare in rheumatology. Introduction An increasing number of children with rheumatic disease reach adulthood thanks to the advances in treatment. A ‘developmentally appropriate’ healthcare and improving patients’ autonomy are critical to meet the needs of the patients transitioning from childhood to adulthood [ 1 ]. Thus, transitional care (TC) including the shift from pediatric to adult-oriented healthcare has attracted more attention during the past decades in rheumatology, as in other areas of medicine. The transition process is defined as the ‘purposeful, planned movement of adolescents and young adults (AYAs) with chronic physical and medical conditions from child-centred to adult-oriented healthcare systems’ [ 2 ]. Transfer, on the other hand, refers to the time of change in the department of care [ 3 ]. Although the number of studies on TC in rheumatology has been increasing, there are still unmet needs in this area [ 4 ]. The most appropriate age for starting TC remains undetermined. Familial and cultural features also have an impact on the ideal age for transition. A standard tool for evaluating transition/transfer readiness with a predetermined cut-off value is also lacking. Moreover, most centres are still struggling with problems in TC such as inadequate financial resources and paucity/lack of medical staff dedicated to TC [ 3 , 5 ]. In this study, we aimed to analyse the transition readiness, the factors affecting the success of the transition process, and learn about the retrospective view of AYAs about the transition process. Patients and methods Patients This is a prospective study. Pediatric rheumatology patients who were older than 12 years and were being followed at the Pediatric Rheumatology Unit of Hacettepe University, Ankara, Turkey for at least six months were included. Patients younger than 12 years and patients with non-rheumatic diseases were excluded. The Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ) 5.0 was filled out by these patients during their pediatric rheumatology visits between February 2021 and December 2022. Also, the patients transferred from pediatric to adult-oriented rheumatology care during the same period were surveyed after the transfer through telephone interviews to learn about the situation of the patients and the success of the TC. Ethical approval was obtained from the Ethical Committee of Hacettepe University (approval number: GO 21/734). All patients and their parents signed written informed consent forms before participating in this study. Transition process The transition process started with the evaluation of AYAs with TRAQ ( Fig. 1 ). After AYAs filled out TRAQ, they were informed verbally about the transition process. The time for the transfer was decided by the treating pediatric rheumatologist for each patient. So, there was no predetermined age for the time of transfer, although the patients were ≥18 years old at the time of the transfer. The decision for transfer time was made according to the patient’s age, diagnosis, disease activity, treatment, TRAQ scores, specific needs such as the requirement of hospitalization, and individual features such as abilities for self-care. The patients’ medical data and information about the transition readiness were shared with the adult rheumatologists working at the Rheumatology Department of Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, in a Zoom meeting that is routinely held once every month. For a few patients followed by multiple different departments, the physicians from other departments also participated in this meeting to reach a consensus about the management and follow-up of the patient. At this meeting, the time for the first appointment at adult-oriented rheumatology department and the adult rheumatologists who would take care of the patient were determined. The pediatric rheumatologist informed patients about the time, place and the name of the adult rheumatologist. Moreover, the first visit at the adult-oriented rheumatology department was done in the presence of a pediatric rheumatologist. Figure 1. Open in a new tab The transition of adolescents and young adults (AYAs) from pediatric to adult-oriented rheumatology care Post-transfer evaluation The AYAs were interviewed by telephone after they were transferred to the adult-oriented rheumatology department (on September 2023) to evaluate the success of the transition and the current situation of AYAs. During the telephone interview, we asked about the following items: (i) whether she/he saw the adult rheumatologist at least once within the six months after the transfer and she/he missed any appointments at the adult-oriented rheumatology department; (ii) whether she/he could handle the outpatient control visit on her/his own; (iii) the pre- and post-transfer 4-item Morisky Medication Adherence Scale (MMAS-4) score; and (iv) the overall judgment of the transition process with a visual analogue scale (VAS) from 0 to 10 (0 being the worst; 10 being the best). Also, the disease activity (active or inactive) at the time of the transfer was noted by the pediatric rheumatologists. The tools to evaluate transition readiness and transition process The Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ) 5.0 [ 6 , 7 ] was used to evaluate patients. The TRAQ validated in Turkish has 20 items at five different domains as managing medications (four items), appointment keeping (seven items), tracking health issues (four items), talking with providers (two items), and managing daily activities (three items) ( Supplementary File S1 , available at Rheumatology online). Each item is assigned to a five-point Likert-type scale from ‘No, I don’t know how’ to ‘Yes, I always do this when I need to’. An increased transition readiness is reflected with a higher score in TRAQ (min–max: 1–5). We used MMAS-4 [ 8 ] to evaluate drug adherence in the transferred patients. In MMAS-4, each item takes a value of either 1 (no) or 0 (yes) ( Supplementary File S2 , available at Rheumatology online). The sum of the score ranges from 0 to 4 and higher scores reflect a higher adherence. A MMAS-4 score of 0–1 means low adherence, 2–3 medium adherence and 4 means high adherence. The transition success was defined as the presence of at least one outpatient visit at adult-oriented rheumatology care during the first six months of the discharge from pediatric rheumatology [ 9 ], and not missing any appointments after the transfer. Statistical analysis The SPSS version 25.0 (SPSS, Inc., Chicago, IL, USA) is used for statistical analysis. Visual (histogram, probability plots) and analytic (Kolmogorov–Smirnov) methods were used to check the suitability of the numeric variables to normal distribution. Descriptive analyses were presented using proportions or medians, minimum and maximum values. Differences in proportions between groups were evaluated by the χ 2 test or Fisher’s exact test where appropriate. The Mann–Whitney U and Kruskal–Wallis tests were used to compare the non-normally distributed continuous data between different groups. The correlation analysis was performed by using the Spearman test. We used the receiver operating characteristics (ROC) curve to find out the best-performing cut-off value for the TRAQ score associated with transfer readiness. P -values ˂0.05 were considered as significant and the confidence interval (CI) was 95%. Results Between February 2021 and December 2022, 504 TRAQs were filled out by 406 patients (F/M = 1.5; median age at diagnosis 10.7 years). Out of these 406 patients: (i) 322 filled out one TRAQ; (ii) 73 filled out two; (iii) eight filled out three; and (iv) three patients filled out four TRAQs at different time points. The total TRAQ scores and scores of each TRAQ domain are presented in Table 1 . Table 1. The characteristics of the pediatric rheumatology patients who filled out the Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaires (TRAQs) and the TRAQ scores The characteristics, median (min–max) All TRAQs ( n = 504) TRAQs filled out by females ( n = 308) TRAQs filled out by males ( n = 196) P value a Age, years 17.1 (12.0–23.2) 17.4 (12.9–22.1) 17.1 (12.3–23.2) 0.830 Total TRAQ score 4.10 (1.70–5.0) 4.20 (2.10–5.0) 4.0 (1.70–5.0) 0.005 Total score of ‘managing medications’ domain 0.90 (0.20–1.0) 0.90 (0.25–1.0) 0.90 (0.20–1.0) 0.230 Total score of ‘appointment keeping’ domain 0.83 (0.20–1.0) 0.83 (0.23–1.0) 0.80 (0.20–1.0) 0.300 Total score of ‘tracking health issues’ domain 0.70 (0.20–1.0) 0.70 (0.20–1.0) 0.65 (0.20–1.0) 0.020 Total score of ‘talking with providers’ domain 1.0 (0.20–1.0) 1.0 (0.20–1.0) 1.0 (0.20–1.0) 0.050 Total score of ‘managing daily activities’ domain 1.0 (0.20–1.0) 1.0 (0.20–1.0) 0.87 (0.33–1.0) <0.001 Open in a new tab a These P values are for the comparison between girls and boys. TRAQ: Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire. The median total TRAQ score (4.2 vs 4.0, respectively; P = 0.005) and the median scores at the domains of ‘tracking health issues’ and ‘managing daily activities’ were higher in TRAQs filled out by females than those filled out by males (0.70 vs 0.65, P = 0.020 and 1 vs 0.870, P < 0.001; respectively). The total TRAQ score was positively but weakly correlated with age (r = 0.28, P < 0.001). When we analysed the correlation of age with the scores of different TRAQ domains, the correlation was most prominent with the domain of ‘managing medications’ (r = 0.30, P < 0.001), and there was no correlation between age and ‘talking with providers’ and ‘managing daily activities’ domains ( Supplementary Table S1 , available at Rheumatology online). The correlation between the total TRAQ score and the duration of follow-up at the pediatric rheumatology department was negligible (r = 0.10, P = 0.020) and there was no correlation between the total TRAQ scores and the age at diagnosis, either ( P = 0.970). We separated TRAQs according to the patients’ diagnoses into six different subgroups as follows: juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) ( n = 106; 21.2%), familial Mediterranean fever (FMF) ( n = 178; 35.3%), systemic autoimmune diseases [systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), juvenile dermatomyositis (JDM), mixed connective tissue disease (MCTD)] ( n = 43; 8.5%), primary systemic vasculitis ( n = 36; 7.1%), more than one inflammatory diseases ( n = 33; 6.5%), and others ( n = 108; 21.4%). The list of the diagnoses is shown in the Supplementary Table S2 , available at Rheumatology online. The median TRAQ scores of patients in different diagnostic groups did not differ significantly ( P = 0.370) ( Supplementary Table S3 , available at Rheumatology online). Between February 2021 and December 2022, 93 out of these 406 patients were transferred from pediatric rheumatology to adult-oriented rheumatology care. The transition process was successful for 78 (83.9%) patients ( Table 2 ). Table 2. The characteristics of pediatric rheumatology patients who were transferred to adult rheumatology care The characteristics, n (%) or median (min-max) All transferred patients ( n = 93) Successfully transitioned patients ( n = 78) The patients whose transition was not successful ( n = 15) P value a Sex, female 55 (59.1) 46 (59) 9 (60) 0.940 Age at the time of transfer, years 19.3 (17.9–23.2) 19.3 (17.9–23.2) 19.1 (17.9–23) 0.880 Age at diagnosis, years 11.9 (1.1–17.3) 11.4 (1.1–17.3) 12.6 (2.4–16.8) 0.450 The duration of pediatric rheumatology F/U, months 76.1 (6.3–210) 78.6 (6.3–203.4) 72.8 (14–210) 0.890 F/U at ≥2 departments 19 (20.5) 15 (19.2) 4 (26.7) 0.510 Active disease at transfer 13 (14) 10 (12.8) 3 (20) 0.430 Time of the evaluation (months later than transfer) 18.2 (7.9–27) 17.5 (7.9–27) 20.3 (8.1–27) 0.001 Total TRAQ score b 4.40 (1.70–5.0) 4.40 (1.70–5.0) 4.50 (3.90–4.90) 0.200 Score of ‘managing medications’ domain b 0.95 (0.20–1.0) 0.95 (0.20–1.0) 0.95 (0.80–1.0) 0.450 Score of ‘appointment keeping’ domain b 0.89 (0.20–1.0) 0.86 (0.20–1.0) 0.89 (0.69–1.0) 0.270 Score of ‘tracking health issues’ domain b 0.80 (0.20–1.0) 0.80 (0.20–1.0) 0.80 (0.40–1.0) 0.430 Score of ‘talking with providers’ domain b 1.0 (0.60–1.0) 1.0 (0.60–1.0) 1.0 (0.70–1.0) 0.980 Score of ‘managing daily activities’ domain b 1.0 (0.33–1.0) 1.0 (0.33–1.0) 1.0 (0.47–1.0) 0.480 Open in a new tab a These P values are for the comparison between successfully transitioned patients and patients whose transition was not successful. b These TRAQ scores belong to the last available TRAQ of the patients before the transfer. F/U: follow-up; TRAQ: Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire. The success of the transition process did not differ significantly according to the patients’ ages, diagnoses, disease activity at the time of transfer, or the number of departments following the patient ( Table 2 ; Supplementary Table S4 , available at Rheumatology online). Also, the median TRAQ scores did not differ significantly between successfully transitioned patients and patients whose transition was not successful ( Table 2 ). However, the median VAS regarding the evaluation of the transition process was lower and the post-transfer MMAS-4 score was worse (8 vs 9, P = 0.030 and 3 vs 4, P = 0.020; respectively) in the latter group ( Table 3 ). It is noteworthy that the MMAS-4 score was worse after transfer in 15.4% of successfully transitioned patients, while this percentage was 40% for patients whose transition was not successful. Table 3. The results of the telephone survey regarding the evaluation of the transition process ( n = 93) The characteristics, n (%) or median (min-max) All transferred patients ( n = 93) The patients whose transition was successful ( n = 78) The patients whose transition was not successful ( n = 15) P value a The duration between transfer and evaluation of the transfer, months 18.2 (7.9–27) 17.5 (7.9–27) 20.3 (8.1–27) 0.001 The pre-transfer MMAS-4 score 4 (2–4) 4 (2–4) 4 (2–4) 0.300 The pre-transfer adherence status High adherence 64 (68.8) 55 (70.5) 9 (60) 0.540 Medium adherence 29 (31.2) 23 (29.5) 6 (40) 0.540 The post-transfer MMAS-4 score 4 (0–4) 4 (1–4) 3 (0–4) 0.020 The post-transfer adherence status High adherence 63 (67.7) 56 (71.8) 7 (46.7) 0.070 Medium adherence 24 (25.8) 19 (24.4) 5 (33.3) 0.520 Low adherence 6 (6.5) 3 (3.8) 3 (20) 0.050 Active disease at the time of transfer 13 (14) 10 (12.8) 3 (20) 0.430 Being able to handle the out-patient controls on his/her own 80 (80.6) 69 (88.5) 11 (73.3) 0.210 VAS b 9 (4–10) 9 (4–10) 8 (4–10) 0.030 Open in a new tab a P values are for the comparison between successfully and unsuccessfully transitioned patients. b Visual analogue scale for evaluating the transition process; 0, the worst; 10, the best. MMAS-4: Morisky Medication Adherence Scale with four items. The median TRAQ score of the transferred patients was significantly higher than the scores of the rest of the study group (not-yet-transferred patients) (4.4 vs 4.0, respectively; P < 0.001). In the ROC analysis, the best performing cut-off value was 4.0 for TRAQ (area under curve 0.649, standard error 0.032, P < 0.001, 95% CI 0.587–0.711) with a sensitivity of 71% and specificity of 48%, for predicting transfer readiness. Discussion This is the largest prospective cohort study analysing the performance of TRAQ and one of the largest studies evaluating transitional care in rheumatology. Also, we attempted for the first time in the literature to find out a cut-off value for TRAQ associated with transfer readiness. The total TRAQ scores were positively correlated with age and higher in the TRAQs filled out by females than males. The best-performing cut-off value for TRAQ associated with transfer readiness was >4.0. In our study, girls performed better than boys in TRAQ similar to several previous studies [ 10–14 ]. It is noteworthy that some researchers failed to demonstrate a significant difference in the TRAQ scores depending on sex [ 15–18 ]. This may be affected by the social structure of the community where the study is performed. It is not surprising to observe a correlation of the total TRAQ score with age. The results of the previous studies [ 12 , 13 , 16–20 ] are also in line with our findings. This correlation was most prominent in the domain of managing medications. The most appropriate age for the start of transition is an area of major debate in TC. The transition guidelines generally suggest an onset age of 13–14 years [ 21 ]. In Turkey, most pediatric rheumatology centres follow their patients until the age of 18 years, and the transfer of care occurs between the ages of 18 and 23 years. Considering this, 15 years of age seems to be an adequate age for the start of transition in our setting. However, it may not be appropriate to determine a standard age. Instead, this age should be flexible and could change according to the social structure of the country and the individual needs of the patient. Although TRAQ is the most frequently used tool for evaluating transition readiness, there is no predetermined cut-off value indicating that the patient is ready for transfer. In our study, 93 patients were picked to be transfer-ready by the treating pediatric rheumatologists during the study period. The decision about the time of the transfer is made according to different factors such as the age of the patient, the general perception of the treating pediatric rheumatologist about the patient’s abilities of self-management, the patient’s diagnosis, and disease activity. When the decision of the treating pediatric rheumatologist was used as a gold standard, a TRAQ score of >4.0 had an acceptable performance to predict transfer readiness in our study, although its specificity was quite low. It is important to mention that the decision about the time of the transfer is also affected by the restrictions caused by hospital regulations. For instance, it is not possible to hospitalize a >18-year-old patient in the pediatric ward in our hospital. Thus, we had to transfer a few patients when we needed to hospitalize them although the patient might not be in his/her best state to be transferred at that time. This may be a factor decreasing the performance of the TRAQ cut-off value. Based on our analysis, we could say that TRAQ scores below 4.0 may indicate that the patient requires more time in pediatric rheumatology care to improve his/her autonomy. However, it is not possible to conclude that the patient’s transfer will definitely be successful if her/his TRAQ score is over 4.0, considering there was no significant difference in total TRAQ scores between successfully and unsuccessfully transferred patients in our study. In our study, the transition success was quite high (∼85%). Having a structured transition plan and transferring patients to an adult-oriented rheumatology department within the same centre were probably the main determinants of this success rate. Improving this rate will probably be possible by addressing the deficiencies in our TC such as the absence of a written TC policy or an established TC education program for AYAs. Interestingly, the transition success was not affected significantly by the patient’s diagnosis, disease activity, the number of departments following the patients, total TRAQ scores, age at diagnosis or the duration of follow-up at the pediatric rheumatology department in our study. However, we might have failed to find out a significant impact based on the limited number of patients, especially in the subgroup where the transition was not successful ( n = 15). Hazel et al. [ 22 ] demonstrated that inactive disease at the time of transfer (zero active joints) and male sex were associated with unsuccessful transfer in JIA patients from pediatric to adult-oriented healthcare. Van Pelt et al. [ 23 ] also showed that low disease activity in JIA was associated with a higher rate of drop-out during transition. The successfully transferred patients rated the transition process with a higher VAS compared with unsuccessfully transitioned patients. This supports the idea that having a good TC experience contributes to the success of the transition. Another finding was that the post-transfer medication adherence was worse in unsuccessfully transferred patients than the successfully transferred ones. This underscores the importance of transitional care for providing a continuum in AYAs’ healthcare. Similar to our findings, Chang et al. [ 24 ] demonstrated that hydroxychloroquine adherence was worst among AYAs who were lost to follow-up during transfer to adult-oriented healthcare. A striking finding in our study was that the medication adherence got worse in some patients even though their transition was successful. In line with our findings, Lotstein et al. [ 25 ] showed that the transfer from pediatric to adult-oriented healthcare itself was associated with worse blood glucose control in juvenile diabetes mellitus patients. During childhood, usually parents take the primary responsibility of giving medications to their children. This responsibility is transferred from parents to AYAs in the period of transition. Thus, monitoring medication adherence is very critical during vulnerable times such as the transition and early post-transfer periods. The main limitation of this study was the limited number of patients in the subgroup where the transition was not successful. This rendered the adequacy of some comparisons. Also, we did not use the most current version of the TRAQ which is TRAQ 6.0–20 items [ 26 ]. However, the most current version was TRAQ 5.0 [ 7 ], and it was the only TRAQ validated in Turkish [ 6 ] when we started our study in February 2021. TRAQ 6.0 was published in the mid-2021 [ 26 ] after we had already gathered many forms. Another limitation was that the decision for the time of transfer was made subjectively by the treating pediatric rheumatologist, and there was no standard procedure. The absence of parent evaluation could also be listed among the limitations of the present study. Lastly, the pre-transfer MMAS-4 was administered to the AYAs at the time of post-transfer telephone interview. So, there may be recall bias. In conclusion, this study is one of the largest prospective cohort studies evaluating transitional care in rheumatology, analysing 504 TRAQ scores and the performance of the transitional care in around ∼95 transferred patients during a period of ∼2 years. The transition success was as high as 85%. The best-performing threshold of TRAQ was >4.0 associated with transfer readiness in our study. Having a good TC experience contributes to the success of transition. However, medication adherence should be monitored closely not only in patients whose transition is not successful but also in the successfully transitioned patients. Supplementary material Supplementary material is available at Rheumatology online. Supplementary Material keae254_Supplementary_Data keae254_supplementary_data.zip (42.3KB, zip) Acknowledgements We wish to thank Ozan Uysal and Yesim Yilmaz for their contribution during the transfer of TRAQ data to the SPSS file. Contributor Information Ezgi Deniz Batu, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Zeynep Balik, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Seher Sener, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Emil Aliyev, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Yagmur Bayindir, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Veysel Cam, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Muserref Kasap Cuceoglu, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Ummusen Kaya Akca, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Levent Kilic, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Internal Medicine, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Ozge Basaran, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Yelda Bilginer, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Sule Apras Bilgen, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Internal Medicine, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Seza Ozen, Division of Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Hacettepe University Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. Data availability The entire dataset used for this study may be available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Funding No specific funding was received from any bodies in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors to carry out the work described in this article. Disclosure statement: The authors declared no conflicts of interests. References 1. Coyne I, Sheehan A, Heery E, While AE.. Healthcare transition for adolescents and young adults with long-term conditions: qualitative study of patients, parents and healthcare professionals'' experiences. J Clin Nurs 2019;28:4062–76. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Blum RWM, Garell D, Hodgman CH. et al. Transition from child-centered to adult health-care systems for adolescents with chronic conditions. A position paper of the Society for Adolescent Medicine. J Adolesc Health 1993;14:570–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Miyamae T, Bando Y.. Transition of patients with childhood-onset rheumatic diseases to adult medical care in Japan. Intern Med 2022;63:25–9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Şahin N, Sönmez HE, Aktay Ayaz N, Sözeri B.. Addressing the unmet needs of transitional care in juvenile idiopathic arthritis. Rheumatology 2023;63:619–29. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shaw KL, Southwood TR, McDonagh JE; British Paediatric Rheumatology Group. Developing a programme of transitional care for adolescents with juvenile idiopathic arthritis: results of a postal survey. Rheumatology 2003;43:211–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Kiziler E, Yildiz D, Fidanci BE.. Validation of transition readiness assessment questionaire in turkish adolescents with diabetes. Balkan Med J 2018;35:93–100. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Wood DL, Sawicki GS, Miller MD. et al. The Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ): its factor structure, reliability, and validity. Acad Pediatr 2014;14:415–22. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Morisky DE, Green LW, Levine DM.. Concurrent and predictive validity of a self-reported measure of medication adherence. Med Care 1986;24:67–74. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Andemariam B, Owarish-Gross J, Grady J. et al. Identification of risk factors for an unsuccessful transition from pediatric to adult sickle cell disease care. Pediatr Blood Cancer 2014;61:697–701. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Sönmez HE, Koç R, Karadağ ŞG, Aktay Ayaz N.. The readiness of pediatric rheumatology patients and their parents to transition to adult-oriented treatment. Int J Rheum Dis 2021;24:397–401. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Lazaroff SM, Meara A, Tompkins MK, Peters E, Ardoin SP.. How do health literacy, numeric competencies, and patient activation relate to transition readiness in adolescents and young adults with rheumatic diseases? Arthritis Care Res 2019;71:1264–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Chapados P, Aramideh J, Lamore K. et al. Getting ready for transition to adult care: tool validation and multi-informant strategy using the Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire in pediatrics. Child Care Health Dev 2021;47:645–53. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Hart LC, Arvanitis M, Sawicki GS. et al. Trajectories of the transition readiness assessment questionnaire among youth with inflammatory bowel disease. J Clin Gastroenterol 2022;56:805–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Rhee H, Choi Y, Tumiel-Berhalter L.. Transition readiness in middle and older adolescents with asthma and associated factors: a descriptive study. J Asthma 2023;60:991–9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Lazarevic D, Dordevic S, Novakovic D, Zecevic M, Susic G.. Transition readiness in adolescents with juvenile idiopathic arthritis and their parents: our single-center experience. Arch Rheumatol 2022;37:411–6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Anelli CG, Len CA, Terreri M, Russo GCS, Reiff AO.. Translation and validation of the Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ). J Pediatr (Rio J) 2019;95:180–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Kittivisuit S, Lerkvaleekul B, Soponkanaporn S, Ngamjanyaporn P, Vilaiyuk S.. Assessment of transition readiness in adolescents in Thailand with rheumatic diseases: a cross-sectional study. Pediatr Rheumatol Online J 2021;19:101. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Oberoi AR, Patterson A, Sobota A.. Measuring transition readiness in adolescents and young adults with sickle cell disease using the transition readiness assessment questionnaire. Pediatr Blood Cancer 2022;69:e29417. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Cleverley K, Davies J, Allemang B, Brennenstuhl S.. Validation of the Transition Readiness Assessment Questionnaire (TRAQ) 5.0 for use among youth in mental health services. Child Care Health Dev 2023;49:248–57. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. van Gaalen MAC, van Gijn E, van Pieterson M. et al. Validation and reference scores of the transition readiness assessment questionnaire in adolescent and young adult IBD patients. J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr 2023;77:381–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Willis ER, McDonagh JE.. Transition from children''s to adults'' services for young people using health or social care services (NICE Guideline NG43). Arch Dis Child Educ Pract Ed 2018;103:253–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Hazel E, Zhang X, Duffy CM, Campillo S.. High rates of unsuccessful transfer to adult care among young adults with juvenile idiopathic arthritis. Pediatr Rheumatol Online J 2010;8:2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. van Pelt PA, Dolhain R, Kruize AA. et al. Disease activity and dropout in young persons with juvenile idiopathic arthritis in transition of care: a longitudinal observational study. Clin Exp Rheumatol 2018;36:163–8. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Chang JC, Knight AM, Lawson EF.. Patterns of healthcare use and medication adherence among youth with systemic lupus erythematosus during transfer from pediatric to adult care. J Rheumatol 2021;48:105–13. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Lotstein DS, Seid M, Klingensmith G. et al. ; for the SEARCH for Diabetes in Youth Study Group. Transition from pediatric to adult care for youth diagnosed with type 1 diabetes in adolescence. Pediatrics 2013;131:e1062–70. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Johnson K, McBee M, Reiss J, Livingood W, Wood D.. TRAQ Changes: improving the measurement of transition readiness by the transition readiness assessment questionnaire. J Pediatr Nurs 2021;59:188–95. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials keae254_Supplementary_Data keae254_supplementary_data.zip (42.3KB, zip) Data Availability Statement The entire dataset used for this study may be available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',36,'2025-10-15 08:58:39','2025-10-15 08:58:39');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3801,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5584081','Exploring patient safety culture in preventive medicine settings: an experience from Northern Italy.','Tereanu C, Sampietro G, Sarnataro F, Mazzoleni G, Pesenti B, Sala LC, Cecchetti R, Arvati M, Brioschi D, Viscardi M, Prati C, Sala G, Barbaglio GG.','2017','inseparable lhas lhas sociologists collaborates lhas piedmont piedmont progetto iride repubblica rete imparando dagli errori verso cultura sicurezza pazienti utenti secretaries psychometrics the12 psychometrics composites composites composites ticked worded composites composites benchmarked actuarial clerk chemist composites composites punitive a11 overlooks a13 f9r c6r f10 f2r f6r f3r cracks f5r f7r f11r punitive a8r a12r a16r worded composites italic b3r shortcuts composites punitive italic clerk punitive composites composites composites composites composites composites composites composites punitive punitive psychometrics composites punitive composites referential autocontrol contrarily piedmont psychometrically psychometrically referentiality bagnasco alass patronage progetto iride cosmi vecchio vecchio cosmi aziende sanitarie bodur feliz mzp035 tabrizchi sedaghat sonon calid asist cali s1134 282x cali salvatore tasselli marzuillo massimi vito belvis federici villari panunzio caporizzi cela antoniciello alonzo bonaccorsi chioffi guberti cairella giostra faggioli pubbl signorelli ricco odone giraldi mannocci saulle maurici capozzi damiani specchia capizzi pubbl sorra nieva westat patientsafetyculture pscintusers søfteland eide nortvedt aase harthug crosscountries hedskold pukk harenstam lindh soop øvretveit hsopc christiaans dingelhoff wal groenewegen manser occelli quenon kret domecq delaperche claverie castets amalberti auroy parneix mzt047 mzt047 eiras escoval grillo fortes assur ijhcqa robida mzt040 brborovic sklebar brborovic brumen sarac flin mearns waterson stride hignett kigawa hsops najjar hamdan baillien vleugels euwema sermeus bruyneel vanhaecht palestinian moghri saari nateqi forooshani ghiasvand sohrabi goudarzi farsi bagnasco tibaldi chirone chiaranda panzone tangolo aleo lazzarino sasso bagnasco vignolo addeo grugnetti calza maricchio sasso cultura sicurezza paziente indagine oncologica infermiere ipasvi rivista linfermiere rivista linfermiere articolo ulss comitato esecutivo aziendale sicurezza paziente annuale sicurezza paziente dmo ulss20 document_library get_file uuid f74fe553 29f4 4a18 87aba1ce55ede59c groupid tereanu sampietro sarnataro mazzoleni pesenti cecchetti arvati brioschi experimenting mzx014 mzx014 patientsafetyculture mcalearney pennell 8pii bodina demarchi castaldi','Summary Introduction. Patient safety and quality in healthcare are inseparable. Examining patient safety culture in staff members contributes to further develop quality in healthcare. In Italy there has been some experience in assessing patient safety culture in staff working in hospital. In this pilot study we explored patient safety culture in public health staff working in Italian Local Health Authorities. Methods. We carried out a descriptive cross sectional study in four Italian territorial Prevention facilities in Northern Italy. We administrated an adapted Italian version of the US Hospital Survey of Patient Safety Culture to all the staff within these facilities. The survey consisted of 10 dimensions based on 33 items, according to the results of a previous psychometric validation. Results. Seventy per cent of the staff responded to the survey (N = 479). Overall, six out of the 10 dimensions exhibited composite scores of positive response frequency for patient safety culture below 50%. While "communication openness" (65%) was the most developed factor, "teamwork across Units" (37%) was the least developed. The work areas with the highest composite scores were Management and the Public Health Laboratory, while in terms of professional categories, Physicians had the highest scores. Patient safety culture in the staff participating in this study was lower than in hospital staff. Discussion. Our descriptive cross sectional study is the first to be carried out in Preventive medicine settings in Italy. It has clearly indicated the need of improvement. Consequently, several interventions with this aim have been implemented. Key words: Patient safety, Staff culture, Territorial preventive care, Italy Introduction Patient safety, defined as "the prevention of harm caused by errors of commission and omission", is a critical challenge of healthcare systems around the world [ 1 ]. Risk profile in healthcare settings depends on a lot of factors, of which users'' characteristics and organizational variables are the most important [ 2 ]. Users'' characteristics, such as age and current health condition, establish the access point to the healthcare system, but they cannot be largely influenced in order to increase patient safety [ 2 ]. For instance, objectively healthy people are served by preventive medicine facilities; community members with less serious conditions are in charge of primary care settings; people with serious acute conditions needing high diagnostic and therapeutic technologies make use of hospitals, while frail elderly people with chronic diseases and lower need of medical technologies are hosted in nursing homes. Conversely, organizational factors such as procedures, staff competence and skills, quality systems and organizational culture, which can be influenced, should be systematically assessed and improved to continually increase patient safety. Quality of care and patient safety should be guaranteed in all access points to the healthcare system of a country, independently of the intensity of care needed by their health condition. Examining staff attitudes with regard to patient safety (safety culture) in each type of healthcare setting may contribute to the better understanding of performance variations across them in terms of quality and safety. Several international surveys showed that differences in patient safety culture exist between primary care, hospital and nursing home staff [ 3 - 5 ]. Surveys of patient safety culture that include territorial Preventive medicine staff are scarce and no disaggregated/specific data are available [ 6 ]. Across Italy, 154 regional public agencies called Local Health Authorities (LHAs) manage healthcare services for subsets of the regional population in defined geographical areas (the average population served is 390.000 inhabitants) [ 7 ]. Within the LHAs in Northern Italy, the Department of Medical Prevention works according to regional prevention programs to provide sanitary education, healthy life style promotion, vaccinations, screenings, safety and hygiene services for food, the environment, the workplace etc., infectious disease surveillance and public health lab analyses [ 8 - 11 ]. Staff work in multidisciplinary teams of public health professionals and workers, including doctors (e.g. specialists in Public Health, Preventive medicine, Infectious diseases, Environmental epidemiology, Toxicology), sanitary assistants (e.g. assistant medical officers, public health nurses), technicians (e.g. environmental health officers or public health inspectors), clerks and others (e.g. psychologists, dietitians and nutritionists, engineers, public health lawyers, sociologists) [ 12 ]. The Department of Medical Prevention closely collaborates with the Department of Veterinary Prevention, according to the "one health" pattern, based on a socio-ecological system perspective, in which several distinct service providers contribute to public health in their catchment area in a coordinated manner, each overseeing a different branch. While in some Italian LHAs medical and veterinary preventive activities are provided by separate departments (e.g. Lombardy region), in others these activities are provided by the same department, i.e. the Department of Prevention (e.g. Piedmont region). The aim of this pilot study was to examine patient safety culture in Italian territorial Prevention facilities by investigating this in four different settings across Northern Italy. Assuming that patient safety culture in the staff members of an organization is a multidimensional concept, we applied the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) Hospital Survey on Patient Safety Culture (Hospital SOPS) [ 13 ], which was translated into Italian and adapted to our target settings, to find out which areas of patient safety were poor and needed improvement. We also examined differences across work areas and professional categories. Methods Study design and Settings A descriptive cross-sectional study was carried out in four Italian territorial Prevention facilities: three Departments of Medical Prevention in the Lombardy region and one Department of Prevention in the Piedmont region. These settings voluntarily participated in an international project aimed at developing patient safety culture in Italian territorial Prevention facilities and Eastern European hospitals ( il progetto IRIDE: Italia - Romania - Repubblica Moldova in Rete: Imparando dagli errori verso una cultura della sicurezza dei pazienti/utenti ). They serve a population varying from about 200,000 to 1,100,000 inhabitants. Data were collected with an online form. The survey was administered from October 1st 2013 until the end of the month in two settings and between September 15th through to October 15th 2014 in the other two settings. Two reminders were sent in each setting before ending data collection, in order to increase the response rate. Participants The target population was represented by all Units and staff members in the target facilities. Most Units existed in all four facilities at the time of the study ( e.g. Hygiene and Public Health, Infectious Disease Prevention and Epidemiology, Community Preventive Medicine, Prevention and Safety in the Workplace, Plant engineering and Safety in the Workplace, Food & Nutrition Hygiene and Management). Some other Units ( e.g. Public Health Laboratory, Environmental Medicine, Legal Medicine and Veterinary) did not exist in all facilities. The main professional categories were represented by Technicians, followed by Physicians, Nurses/Sanitary assistants and Unit assistants/clerks/secretaries. Data sources and measurement Since its release in 2004, the AHRQ Hospital SOPS was translated in 31 languages and administered in 66 countries [ 14 ]. It showed acceptable psychometric properties in Europe [ 15 - 24 ], Asia and the Middle East [ 25 - 27 ]. It had been already translated into Italian (with the back translation method) [ 28 ] and applied in several Italian hospitals [ 28 , 29 , 30 ]. It was slightly adjusted for application in our settings and pre-tested on a small group of staff members from different professions. Psychometrics of the Italian version of the Hospital SOPS for territorial Prevention facilities were then explored. Among the 42 items of the12-factor original US survey, only 33 items based on a 10-factor model showed acceptable psychometrics for application in our target facilities [ 31 ]. Moreover, the survey assessed two output indicators and required additional information on work area, staff position, and whether they have direct or indirect interaction with patients. The survey also allowed for open comments to be written at the end. Items were measured using a 5-point Likert-type scale and were then aggregated into 10 composites (factors). Most safety culture composites used the scale of response option in terms of agreement ( Strongly agree to Strongly disagree ) and three composites in terms of frequency ( Always to Never ). Patient safety grade (output indicator) was measured with a five-point scale ranging from " Excellent " to " Failing ". Another output indicator was the number of reported adverse events in the last 12 months, assessed through five frequency categories. Participants were asked to respond to this item only if there had been an incident reporting system in their facility. Anonymity was ensured throughout the study. To reduce respondents'' fear of being identified, several methods were adopted. Units with very low number of staff were aggregated to Units with higher number of staff, within the same work area. Moreover, a work area called " Not otherwise specified " was added to the seven work areas obtained, in order to be ticked by the respondents who did not want to indicate his/her true Unit. The same was done for the professional categories. The pre-test participants were informed that they would not be further invited to complete the survey. A thorough quality check was carried out on the surveys received. Forms with less than one entire section completed, with less than half the questions answered, and straight-lining forms (where responses to all items in Sections A, B, C, D and F were the same) were excluded. Study size Overall, 673 workers received the survey (staff census). After the quality check, the final dataset consisted of 479 respondents across four territorial prevention facilities. Statistical methods Analyses were performed using STATA. The percentage of missing data was very low and therefore it was not necessary to address this issue. Frequency distributions were computed for the demographic characteristics of the respondents, for the two output indicators of the survey, as well as for the responses to each one of the 33 items of the survey. Negatively worded items were reverse coded before calculating the 10 composites scores. Patient safety culture was measured overall, by work area, and by professional category. Overall composites were benchmarked with Italian and US hospitals and other outpatient settings from other countries [ 3 , 4 , 6 , 28 , 32 ]. In order to facilitate comparisons, 95% confidence intervals (CI) were computed. Results The response rate across the four territorial Prevention facilities varied from 67% to 73%, with an average of 71%. All items had good variability and the rates of missing responses ranged from 0% to 4% per item. Table I shows respondents'' demographics and response distribution concerning two output indicators. Tab. I. General characteristics of the respondents (I) and output indicators (II). Variable Frequency % I. ACTUARIAL CHARACTERISTICS Your Work Area A. Hygiene and Public Health 169 35 B. Workplace Prevention 130 27 C. Food & Nutrition Hygiene 71 15 D. Public Health Laboratory 25 5 E. Legal Medicine 21 4 F. Veterinary Medicine 11 2 G. Management 26 5 H. Not otherwise specified 26 5 Work in the Department (years) < 1 10 2 1-5 59 12 6-10 48 10 11-15 83 17 16-20 63 13 21 or more 216 45 Work in the Unit (years) < 1 15 3 1-5 70 15 6-10 67 14 11-15 99 21 16-20 73 15 21 or more 155 32 Working hours in the Department per week < 20 7 1 20-38 355 74 39-59 110 23 60 or more 7 1 Staff position in the Department Technician 206 43 Physician 84 18 Unit assistant/clerk/secretary 82 17 Nurse/sanitary assistant 74 15 Other (Chemist, Dietician, etc) 33 7 Direct interaction or contact with patients/users Yes 430 90 No 48 10 Experience in the profession (years) < 1 7 1 1-5 32 7 6-10 44 9 11-15 71 15 16-20 65 14 21 or more 259 54 II. OUTPUT INDICATORS Patient Safety Grade Excellent 47 10 Very Good 184 39 Acceptable 223 47 Poor 17 4 Failing 4 1 Number of Events Reported Non response 173 36 None 256 53 1-2 36 8 3-5 8 2 6-10 3 1 11-20 0 0 21 or more 3 1 Open in a new tab Most respondents (35%) worked in the Hygiene and Public Health area, which was common to all four facilities, while the Veterinary Medicine area provided the least amount of respondents (2%), as it existed in one setting only. Half of the respondents experienced more than 21 years in the current profession (54%) and in the department (45%), but only a third of them (32%) in the current Unit, reflecting a job rotation process across the Units of the same department. Most respondents (74%) usually worked between 20 to 38 hours a week. Almost half of the respondents were Prevention Technicians (43%). Ninety percent of the respondents worked in direct contact with patients/users. Half of the respondents (49%) appreciated the "Patient Safety Grade" indicator as "excellent or very good". The most frequent response to the " Number of events reported in the last 12 months " indicator was " No event reports ". Interestingly, 36% of the respondents did not answer it. Table II shows the response frequency and the percentage of positive responses (with 95% CI) by survey item. Positive answers varied from 23% for the item "Problems often occur in the exchange of information across department Units", to 70% for the item "People support one another in this Unit". Composites scores varied from 36% for "handoffs and transitions" to 65% for "communication openness". Six out of 10 composites were poor (i.e. under the cut-off point of 50%). These were: "management support for patient/user safety", "feedback and communication about errors", "frequency of events reported", "non-punitive response to errors", "teamwork across Units", "handoffs and transitions". Tab. II. Hospital SOPS adapted for Italian territorial Prevention facilities: response frequency and percentage of "positive" responses by survey item and composite, with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Composite and survey item (N = 479 respondents) Number of total responses % "Positive" response 1 95%CI 1. Teamwork Within Units 2 1905 59.0 56.7-61.2 A1. People support one another in this Unit. 477 70.4 66.4-74.5 A3. When a lot of work needs to be done quickly, we work together as a team to get the work done. 477 56.6 52.2-61.1 A4. In this Unit people treat each other with respect. 477 56.2 51.7-60.6 A11. When one area in this Unit gets really busy others help out. 474 52.5 48.0-57.0 2. Supervisor/ Head 3 Expectations & Actions Promoting Patient/ User Safety 4 1425 58.6 56.0-61.2 B1. My supervisor/