INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3807,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3483970','Ludwig Teleky (1872-1957): a leader in social and occupational medicine.','Brown TM, Fee E.','2012','teleky admired cofounded gewerbepathologie gewerbehygiene teleky andrija stampar yugoslavian teleky inspector factories rhineland teleky courageously persecution teleky lectureship teleky excerpt amends victimized nazis teleky weindling unification nazism teleky andrija stampar charismatic wulf sozialmediziner teleky gewerbehygiene arbeitsmedizin mabuse terris dualities wulf sozialmediziner teleky baader teleky 85th birthday gewerbepathologie gewerbehygiene','LUDWIG TELEKY WAS A MAJOR international figure in early twentieth century social and occupational medicine, admired for his scientific accomplishments, legislative and regulatory achievements, and progressive political views. 1 He published some 350 papers, monographs, and books and cofounded an important journal, Archiv für Gewerbepathologie und Gewerbehygiene (Archive of Industrial Pathology and Hygiene), yet at the same time held notable administrative positions. He was active in the German Social Democratic Party and played a significant role in international organizations as a founding member of the Permanent International Committee on Industrial Medicine in 1906 and as editor of the Encyclopedia of Industrial Diseases published by the International Labor Organization (founded in 1919). 2 Teleky was born in Vienna, Austria, on July 12, 1872, and graduated in medicine from the University of Vienna in 1896. 2 He was appointed lecturer in social medicine at Vienna in 1909, a position he held for 10 years. 3 In that role he inspired future leaders of social medicine and international health such as the Croatian Andrija Stampar, who went on to play important roles in Yugoslavian public health, the League of Nations Health Organization, and the World Health Organization. 4 In 1921 Teleky became medical inspector for factories for the Rhineland and, simultaneously, director of the Postgraduate Academy of Social Hygiene and Industrial Medicine at Dusseldorf. 5 During his years in Austria, Teleky was respected for his broad views on social medicine and industrial hygiene. 6 During his years in Germany, he was noteworthy for courageously keeping his distance from racial hygiene and eugenics. 7 In 1932, because of Nazi persecution, Teleky was deprived of his German appointments and returned to Vienna. By 1938, it was necessary for him to leave Europe for the United States, where in 1939 he obtained a lectureship at the University of Chicago. In 1944 he took a position with the Labor Division of the New York State Department of Industrial Hygiene. While in New York City, Teleky devoted much of his time to writing and, among other works, published his History of Factory and Mine Hygiene (1948), from which this excerpt is taken. Near the end of his life, the Federal German Republic tried to make amends to him for having been victimized by the Nazis and conferred the Grand Cross of Merit on Dr. Teleky, and the Town of Hamm named a street after him. 8 References 1. Paul Weindling, Health, Race and German Politics Between National Unification and Nazism, 1870 – 1945 (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1989), 215, 218, 223, 225. 2. Anon, “Ludwig Teleky, M.D.” BMJ 2 (1957): 1053. 3. G. Rosen, “What is social medicine? A genetic analysis of the concept,” Bulletin of the History of Medicine 21 (1947): 715–716. [ PubMed ] 4. T.M. Brown, Fee E, “Andrija Stampar: charismatic leader of social medicine and international health,” American Journal of Public Health 96 (2006): 1383. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 5. A. Wulf, “Der sozialmediziner Ludwig Teleky,” (1872-1957) und die Entwicklung der Gewerbehygiene zur Arbeitsmedizin (Frankfurt am Main: Mabuse-Verlag, 2001) 6. M. Terris, “Concepts of health promotion: dualities in public health theory,” Journal of Public Health Policy 13 (1992): 35–36. [ PubMed ] 7. U. Lindner, “Review of Andreas Wulf, der sozialmediziner Ludwig Teleky,” Social History of Medicine 15 (2002): 529. 8. E.W. Baader, “[To Ludwig Teleky on his 85th birthday],” Archiv für Gewerbepathologie und Gewerbehygiene 15 (1957): 539–541. [ PubMed ]',15,'2025-10-15 08:59:14','2025-10-15 08:59:14');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3808,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831986','Understanding the multiple dimensions of ageing: 5Ms for the rheumatologist.','Buehring B, van Onna M, Myasoedova E, Lee J, Makris UE.','2024','5ms 5ms multicomplexity tenants embody 5ms hartford 4ms mentation multicomplexity 5ms 5ms 5ms multicomplexity inflammageing underdiagnosis mimicked overshadowed lonely lonely 5ms underused 5ts 5ms 5ms 5ms 5ms 5ms 5ms prejudices 5ms 5ms ageism familiarise ageism 5ms multicomplexity charlston elixhauser deprescribing forta geriatricscareonline climb stairs bioelectrical elicitation customising medsafer deprescribing deprescribing deprescribing semira deprescribing mentation disproportionally neuropsychologist 5ms anticholinergics handgrip irvin vasculitides climbing stairs bodyweight multicomplexity 5ms equips 5ms orthogeriatric orthogeriatric 5ms 5ms 5ms ageism 5ts 5ms mentation 5ms 5ms 5ms multicomplexity 5ms 5ms 5ms 5ms 5ms multicomplexity deprescribing elicitation originality alexion galapagos dohme genzyme theramex mvo galapagos ag068192 k24 ag078179 r03 ag067975 ag082727 uem hx003350 ag022845 5ts 5tsframework outreachpro ageism combatting ageism ageism inouye studenski kuchel lieber wysham sattui s2665 loon e0213263 hiligsmann beaudart bruyere onna starmans boonen mccue ihi aubert schnipper roumet onna boonen cleutjens fahm boonen aerch onna mgb buehring roul gunderson myasoedova crowson emmett hanenberg sierevelt tulner neth garagnani morsiani makris higashi ageism alami desjeux e19634 ranganath maranian elashoff myasoedova gunderson crowson dalbeth boocock ellingsen giraldi graugaard lonely cacioppo cacioppo capitanio surkalim e067068 juel ekholm lonely lunstad dainty larranaga vetrano masnoon shakib kalisch ellett caughey galloway hyrich bathon jwj petrovsky ðoan loizos e55 rabidou dhar bechman hajjar bowling whitson 5ts ageism postdiagnosis hilmer reeve deprescribing phun cherubini guiteras stopp pazan wehling forta forta myasoedova sattui jto makris s2665 jentoft bahat cawthon densitom iuliano fyfe anzssfr kahler mastarone zuzero elicitation limpawattana phungoen mitsungnern laosuangkoon tansangworn peschken dolovich grymonpre tisseverasinghe makris deprescribing earl katapodis schneiderman shoemaker deprescribing s23 rashidi medsafer deprescribing winthrop buttgereit e368 sarsour petho schramm jick buttgereit bernasconi semira schett kronzer crowson vassilaki myasoedova sheu e0186475 sattui patnode perdue rossom bingham tayer shifman touma caffarra gwinnutt toyoda barraclough verstappen hornberger ruano tayer shifman kopec atiquzzaman nantes dhaliwal colosimo touma rayes tani stubbs epling iqcode matcham hotopf conradi ormel magro checa zirkzee beaart voorde ljj npsle bhangle rosenstein adedire copsey cd012221 leibson tosteson ransom melton leboff greenspan insogna lusardi 990s 91s dodds sayer buehring parvaee noninflammatory jentoft icfsr rausch osthoff niedermann gwinnutt wieczorek balanescu kumthekar moye karel cornelissen pennings giovannetti agostini reuben buehring manu s2665 e211271 bringhen mateos leendert jaa linkens aemjh poeze orthogeriatric','Abstract The global population is ageing and the rheumatology workforce should be prepared to take care of the inevitable complexities of ageing patients. We can learn from our colleagues and experts in geriatrics about how best to manage multimorbidity, polypharmacy, geriatric syndromes, and shifting priorities of older patients in the context of delivering care for rheumatic diseases. One approach to learning and adopting key ageing constructs within rheumatology practice is to incorporate the established Geriatric 5Ms—principles fundamental to caring for older adults. In this Series paper we discuss the 5Ms in the context of rheumatology practice (1) multicomplexity: assessing and managing multimorbidity and challenging biopsychosocial situations, (2) medications: ensuring that medications do not interfere with the other Ms, (3) mind: managing neurocognitive disorders and comorbid mental health conditions, (4) mobility: ensuring older adults can move independently and safely, and (5) what matters most: aligning care with an older adult’s specific goals. Introduction The global population is ageing and the rheumatology community should be prepared to take care of the inevitable complexities of ageing patients. Given the shifting demographics of the global population, there is an urgent need to understand the unique aspects of older adults with rheumatic diseases and to adapt our approaches to optimally manage the complexity of older adults. We can learn from our colleagues and experts in geriatrics about how best to assess, manage, and communicate about multimorbidity, polypharmacy, geriatric syndromes, and shifting priorities of older adults in the context of delivering appropriate rheumatological care. 1 One of the central tenants of the practice of geriatrics and gerontology is treating the whole person rather than focusing solely on individual diseases. Geriatric syndromes embody this philosophy and aim to identify multifactorial conditions that do not fit discrete disease categories common in older adults. These multifactorial conditions are characterised by a set of symptoms and physiological abnormalities that form a single phenomenology and are the final common pathway resulting from accumulated physical, psychosocial, and environmental factors. Examples of geriatric syndromes are immobility and instability, malnutrition, and cognitive impairment. These syndromes often interact and co-occur with each other and are associated with poor outcomes. 2 Frailty also frequently occurs in older adults. There is an ongoing discussion as to whether frailty is a geriatric syndrome, how to best define it, and how to measure different types of frailty. As frailty is both an important and complex topic, it is the focus of a paper in this Series. 3 Geriatric syndromes lead to reduced activity levels and functional status, resulting in reduced participation, independence, and involvement in everyday life. Older adults fear being dependent on others and not being able to do the things they enjoyed in the past. 4 , 5 Since older adults typically age 65 years and older with rheumatic diseases are at increased risk of losing their independence and the ability to participate in social roles, it is possible that they experience these fears more often and earlier than older adults without rheumatic diseases. As rheumatologists, we provide care for a broad spectrum of patients including those who are diagnosed at a younger age with autoimmune inflammatory rheumatic diseases and who later develop age-related or inflammation-related comorbidities, and complex older adults with existing multimorbidity who develop rheumatic diseases at an older age. Management approaches for older adults with rheumatic diseases should take into consideration unique geriatric principles that are the focus of this Series paper. Consideration of the older patient, family and caregiver perspectives, goals, and values are also important for shared medical decision making. 6 Additionally, the importance of interdisciplinary teams and recognising how competing priorities result in additional complexity should be considered by both generalists and specialists. To adequately address the unique health issues of older adults in accordance with guidelines, geriatricians perform the Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA), which is the gold standard for identifying medical, psychosocial, and functional limitations in older adults at high risk of adverse outcomes. 7 Although the CGA is widely used by geriatricians, it is not commonly adopted by rheumatologists due to its time-consuming nature. However, rheumatologists who wish to integrate geriatric principles into their daily practice can use the geriatric 5Ms approach, which is part of the Age-Friendly Health Systems initiative from the John A Hartford Foundation and the Institute for Healthcare Improvement, designed to provide evidence-based care to all older adults. The framework was initially developed as the 4Ms consisting of: what matters, medication, mentation, and mobility, which are elements of high-quality care. 8 Later, multicomplexity was added by the American Geriatrics Society as the fifth component to emphasise the importance of considering the older adult as a whole person with multiple chronic conditions and complex biopsychosocial needs ( figure 1 ). 1 Figure 1: Core components of each of the geriatric 5Ms and the interdisciplinary teams involved. Open in a new tab Components of the Geriatric 5Ms and potential interdisciplinary team members who could be particularly appropriate to partner with for assessing and managing the specific M (in addition to the rheumatologist, the primary care provider, or geriatrician). In this Series paper we discuss each of the 5Ms in the context of older adults with rheumatic diseases. We conclude with implementation considerations and a proposed clinical approach to enhance visibility of these important principles and ultimately to improve rheumatology practice and outcomes for older adults with rheumatic diseases. Multicomplexity Multimorbidity, the coexistence of two or more chronic conditions, is common in older adults with rheumatic diseases. The proinflammatory state that comes with ageing (known as inflammageing) contributes to the development of age-related conditions and geriatric syndromes, such as atherosclerosis, osteoporosis, neurodegenerative disorders, sarcopenia, and frailty, and this process might be further accelerated by the presence of rheumatic diseases. 9 – 12 Several studies found that individuals with rheumatoid arthritis have an increased prevalence and incidence of multimorbidity. 13 , 14 A 2021 population-based study (data were collected between 1999 and 2013) found that 228 (38%) of 597 individuals with rheumatoid arthritis already had multimorbidity at the time of rheumatoid arthritis diagnosis compared with 188 (32%) of 594 of age-matched and sex-matched controls without rheumatoid arthritis. 14 Individuals with rheumatoid arthritis also had a higher likelihood of developing incident multimorbidity during the 10-year follow-up period (hazard ratio [HR] 1·39 [95% CI 1·14–1·69]). 14 Caring for older adults with multimorbidity is challenging due to the complex interplay between multiple medical and psychosocial conditions. Detection of new rheumatic diseases in older adults can be difficult since many can present with non-specific, unusual, or altered symptoms. 12 , 15 In older frail adults, subtle changes in behaviour or function are often the first symptoms indicative of rheumatic diseases. 16 For example, loss of appetite, falling, and malaise, can be the only symptoms of giant cell arteritis (refer to the frailty Series paper). 3 In addition to an insidious disease presentation, older adults, caregivers, and health-care professionals tend to regard symptoms, such as joint pain and stiffness as a normal part of ageing. 17 , 18 Older adults might also be reluctant to report new symptoms because of fear of undergoing burdensome diagnostic tests or facing negative economic, social, and functional implications. 19 However, timely recognition of an atypical disease presentation and discussing the possibility of symptom under-reporting with older adults can prevent misdiagnosis, underdiagnosis, and delayed initiation of treatment. 15 Among older adults with established rheumatic diseases, the disease status is often altered, mimicked, or overshadowed by multimorbidity, geriatric syndromes, and psychosocial factors. 10 An observational cohort study that analysed data from 1584 individuals with rheumatoid arthritis found that increasing comorbidity levels were independently associated with less improvement in the clinical disease activity index after initiation of anti-rheumatic treatment. 20 Conversely, results of common geriatric functional status tests, such as gait speed, can be distorted by pain and joint damage due to the rheumatic disease itself or by conditions that are closely linked to both rheumatic diseases and geriatric syndromes, including fatigue. 21 , 22 Psychosocial wellbeing, such as experiencing meaningful social connections, also has a considerable effect on the clinical status of the underlying rheumatic disease and vice versa in older adults. 23 Loneliness, the feeling of being alone regardless of the amount of social contacts, is strongly related to social isolation—ie, lack of social contact and having few people to interact with regularly. Social isolation can lead to loneliness; however, people can also feel lonely without being socially isolated. 24 The prevalence of loneliness and social isolation varies widely between countries and cultures. While not specific to rheumatic diseases, in a systematic literature review and meta-analyses including data from 30 European countries, the prevalence of loneliness was lowest in northern Europe (4·2–6·5% for adults age ≥60 years) and highest in eastern Europe (18·7–24·2% for adults age ≥60 years). 25 Loneliness and social isolation are associated with poor health behaviours and outcomes including smoking, reduced physical activity, increased risk for cardiovascular disease, cognitive impairment, and higher rates of all-cause mortality. 26 , 27 Studies specifically focusing on loneliness and social isolation in older patients with rheumatic diseases remain scarce. 28 A cross-sectional study that analysed data from 9299 participants older than 50 years included in the English Longitudinal Study of Ageing found that those who had chronic musculoskeletal pain were more likely to be lonely (odds ratio [OR] 1·15 [95% CI 1·01–1·31]), but were less likely to be socially isolated (OR 0·87 [95% CI 0·75–0·99]), than those without pain. 28 When managing older adults with multimorbidity, geriatric syndromes, and poor psychosocial wellbeing, it is important that clinicians understand how these conditions contribute and interact with each other and affect the individual’s physical and psychosocial functioning, and their response to therapy. 29 This framework should be the basis of a personalised management plan that will be further enriched by incorporating the health-related goals, priorities, and needs of the patient, referred to as what matters most in the 5Ms. 30 , 31 Medications Medication use in older adults poses unique challenges owing to underlying multimorbidity, consequent polypharmacy, increased risk of drug–drug interactions, and adverse drug effects with novel or atypical presentation or complications. 32 , 33 Polypharmacy, defined as the concurrent use of five or more medications, is rising in the ageing population because of a higher comorbidity burden and greater compliance with disease-specific guidelines that advocate for use of multiple medications. 32 , 34 , 35 It is important to note, however, there is an absence of age-specific and multimorbidity-specific recommendations as clinical trials that provide the evidence-base for these guidelines often exclude older adults based on their age or the presence of chronic conditions. 36 , 37 Recognising the importance of age-friendly care, there is an increasing acknowledgment of the necessity for representative enrolment of older adults in clinical research. 36 In rheumatoid arthritis, one in two older adults have polypharmacy. 38 In a cohort study of more than 22 000 individuals with rheumatoid arthritis, each additional medication reduced the odds of a good response to biological disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs; adjusted OR 0·97 [95% CI 0·95–0·98]) and increased the risk of adverse effects (adjusted HR 1·06 [95% CI 1·05–1·07]). 38 Additionally, polypharmacy is associated with greater treatment burden, medication non-adherence, geriatric syndromes, and poor quality of life. 39 Several tools including the Medication Regimen Complexity Index have been developed to measure the effect of polypharmacy or exposure to medications with an unfavourable risk profile in older adults ( table ). 44 However, the index remains underused in rheumatology and there are no validated tools to measure the burden or complexity of various DMARD regimens (with or without accounting for non-DMARD-related polypharmacy) and their effect on functioning and wellbeing in older adults with rheumatic diseases. Table: Gaps, implementation challenges, call to action, resources, and next steps Call to action Potential assessments, resources, and next steps to consider Research Lack of age-specific data for older adults with rheumatic diseases who are often excluded from randomised controlled trials Inclusion of older adults in trials Become familiar with and adopt the NIH Inclusion Across Lifespan policy: sponsors (foundations and industry) and organisations or societies (ie, American College of Rheumatology, European Alliance of Associations for Rheumatology, and Pan American League of Associations for Rheumatology) to consider requiring justification for age-based exclusion in studies to assure reflection of the populations we care for; journals cannot influence study design, but can improve presentation of information on the representation of older adults in studies; intentionally plan for and learn specific approaches on how to recruit and retain diverse older adults in research studies; the 5Ts Framework (target population, team, time, tips to accommodate, and tools) that supports the inclusion of older adults in research; 40 utilise NIH resources (eg, OutReach Pro) Additional data needed for older adults with rheumatic disease, for each of the 5Ms Identify the most appropriate measures and variables in electronic health records to capture components of 5Ms See each 5M specific suggestions regarding assessment and resources Resource constraints Limited time during office visits; how to assess and intervene on all of the geriatric 5Ms Assemble appropriate interdisciplinary teams for the 5Ms; recognise that assessing and addressing the 5Ms can be done over multiple visits Build interdisciplinary teams and partner with colleagues in geriatrics, palliative care, physical therapy or occupational therapy, physical medicine and rehabilitation, pharmacy, neurology, and clinical psychology or psychiatry ( figure 1 ); develop effective ways to communicate with colleagues across disciplines and care settings; engage with caregivers to assess and act on 5Ms Knowledge, training, and prejudices Absence of formal training in assessment and intervention for each of the geriatric 5Ms; assumption that older adults are similar to younger adults, therefore they do not need a different approach Acknowledge that older adults with rheumatic diseases are uniquely complex and particularly susceptible to poor outcomes; recognise the importance of geriatric 5Ms for patients with rheumatic diseases; seek out additional training from experts in geriatrics; raise awareness that ageism exists at all levels (ie, individual, interpersonal, institutional, and policy) and affect the care that older adults with rheumatic diseases receive 3 There are multiple educational resources and career development communities and initiatives that focus on enhancing geriatric key principles among medical and surgical specialists (ie, National Institutes on Aging, American Geriatrics Society, The Aging Initiative, American Federation for Aging Research, Claude D Pepper Older Americans Independence Centers, and European Geriatric Medicine Society and its national society members); familiarise with WHO Global Campaign to Combat Ageism 41 Gaps and implementation challenges specifically related to 5Ms Multicomplexity Establish what combination or severity of comorbidities have the greatest implications for rheumatic disease outcomes; identify how the comorbidities influence what matters most Adapt the Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment for rheumatology practice and to fit the needs of patients with rheumatic diseases; 6 comorbidity indices, such as the Charlston–Deyo, Rheumatic Disease Comorbidity Index, multimorbidity weighted index, or Elixhauser comorbidity indices can be integrated into the electronic health record; 8 , 42 existing medical problem lists and review of systems used for documentation in electronic health records already capture many of the comorbidities; simply using the individual’s number of health conditions might be the most pragmatic and practical approach in clinical practice 8 Medications Recognise the importance of medication optimisation across the lifespan, including deprescribing when appropriate 43 Drug Burden Index; Medication Regimen Complexity Index; 44 Medication-Related Burden Quality of Life; Beers Criteria; 45 Screening Tool of Older Persons’ Prescriptions and Screening Tool to Alert to Right Treatment criteria; 46 FORTA; 47 GeriatricsCareOnline.org ; structured medication reconciliation and review Mind Establish how rheumatic diseases might predispose to accelerated or early onset ageing and cognitive impairment (see cognition Series paper); 48 future research agenda on cognitive impairment in rheumatic disease Depressive symptoms instruments (ie, Patient Health Questionnaire-2, Patient Health Questionnaire-9, and NIH Patient-Reported Outcomes Measurement Information System); delirium (ie, confusion assessment method); cognitive impairment (ie, Mini-Cog, Mini-Mental State Examination, Montreal Cognitive Assessment, and American College of Rheumatology Neuropsychological Battery) Mobility Determine how to assess functional status that is not disease specific; incorporate evaluation of body composition factors that influence mobility, functional status, other rheumatic disease-specific outcomes Falls assessment and prevention counselling; strength, assistance with walking, rise from a chair, climb stairs, and falls questionnaire; 49 Short Physical Performance Battery; 49 , 50 gait speed; 49 , 50 grip strength; 49 , 50 timed-up-and-go-test; 49 , 50 dual energy x-ray absorptiometry; 49 , 51 bioelectrical impedance spectroscopy 49 , 51 What matters most Learn techniques to prioritise what matters most and align care plans with patient priorities; establish whether aligning care with what matters most in rheumatology improves outcomes and in which outcomes Learn from and collaborate with experts in geriatrics, palliative care, and psychology; goal elicitation tools have been developed for patients with rheumatoid arthritis; 52 patient priority care is one approach to decision making that involves eliciting patient health priorities and translating them into clinic care 53 Open in a new tab NIH=National Institutes of Health. Atypical presentations of complications from medication use also complicate prescribing for older adults. For example, biological DMARDs increase the risk of infections and older adults with an infection might present with altered mental status and an inability to mount a fever or adequate white cell elevation. 33 , 54 Non-adherence (intentional or non-intentional) and dosage errors contribute to adverse outcomes and might be more common in older adults with complex medication regimens or cognitive impairment. For example, methotrexate is intended for weekly dosing, however, serious harm related to daily intake has been reported. 55 Therefore, when older adults present with clinical features not usually associated with known rheumatic diseases or underlying comorbidities, a thorough medication history should be acquired and interdisciplinary care should be engaged to help distinguish between the effects of a disease and the adverse effects of medication in older adults. In addition to burden and complexity, medication appropriateness is important to consider in older adults with rheumatic diseases. Tools including the Screening Tool of Older Persons’ Prescriptions and Screening Tool to Alert to Right Treatment criteria, Fit For The Aged criteria, and Beers Criteria have been developed to identify clinically important, potentially inappropriate medications that should be avoided in most circumstances or depending on certain drug–disease and drug–drug interactions ( table ). 45 – 47 Antidepressants, benzodiazepines, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, nifedipine, opioids (especially when used with benzodiazepines, gabapentin, or pregabalin), and skeletal muscle relaxants are potentially inappropriate medications that often contribute to polypharmacy in individuals with rheumatic diseases. 56 , 57 Although explicit criteria aid the identification of potentially inappropriate medications, the lack of specificity for rheumatic diseases, or concordance with rheumatology guidelines, limits their utility and implementation in rheumatology practice. For example, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are the preferred first-line therapy for axial spondyloarthritis and analgesics for other arthritis. Thus, customising tools to account for rheumatic disease-specific prescribing yet minimising potentially inappropriate medications might enhance their applicability in usual care and rheumatology. Interventions that incorporate education, explicit screening tools into electronic decision support, and structured interdisciplinary medication reviews have been effective in reducing potentially inappropriate medications and polypharmacy. 58 In the MedSafer cluster randomised study involving 5698 hospitalised participants with a median age of 78 (IQR 72–85) years, potentially inappropriate medications were stopped in 1249 (55·4%) of 2256 participants in the intervention group that received personalised deprescribing reports and 795 (29·8%) of 2667 participants in the usual care group (medication reconciliation). 59 Deprescribing refers to the careful and systematic approach to lowering doses or discontinuing medications that might be harmful and ensuring the process is safe, effective, centred on the patient’s needs, and aligned with their health goals. 43 , 57 , 58 The principle of deprescribing, which involves continuous assessment of medication benefits versus harms, is relevant to rheumatoid arthritis clinical guidelines that recommend minimising the use of glucocorticoids and tapering DMARDs when feasible. 34 , 35 Glucocorticoids are commonly used in rheumatoid arthritis and their use is associated with duration-dependent and dose-dependent increases in the risk of diabetes, osteoporosis, fractures, and serious infections. 60 – 62 This medication-induced comorbidity requiring pharmacotherapy (eg, bisphosphonate for the treatment of glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis) compounds the burden of polypharmacy in older adults. The SEMIRA (Steroid EliMination in Rheumatoid Arthritis) trial 53 involving 259 individuals with rheumatoid arthritis on tocilizumab and long-term glucocorticoids with stable low-disease activity, showed that 85 (64·9%) of 131 participants in the prednisone taper group were able to taper glucocorticoids without flare or signs of adverse drug withdrawal effects. Incorporating educational interventions proven effective in deprescribing potentially inappropriate medications might facilitate glucocorticoid tapering, which could reduce polypharmacy to manage glucocorticoid side-effects and enhance use of DMARDs in older adults. In addition to glucocorticoids, some patients with rheumatoid arthritis in remission might reduce their DMARDs including biologics, although sustained drug-free remission is questionable due to the absence of data. 64 Understanding when, which, and how to taper glucocorticoids and DMARDs could provide the opportunity to reduce the risk of polypharmacy and achieve more tailored treatment approaches. When prescribing for older adults, physicians (and if accessible, clinical pharmacists) should ensure that older adults with rheumatic diseases are appropriately screened and managed for polypharmacy, comorbidities, and adverse medication effects. Moreover, older adults should be routinely and frequently re-examined to ensure that the benefits of treatment outweigh its harm, and management approaches align with the patients’ goals and preferences. Mind or mentation Increased risk of cognitive impairment and dementia in individuals with rheumatic diseases, although with variable magnitude, has been shown in several populations. 65 , 66 As exemplified by studies of patients with rheumatoid arthritis, the use of DMARDs, particularly biologics, is associated with reduced risk of dementia. 67 Data on the effects of DMARDs on the risk of cognitive impairment in other rheumatic diseases is absent. While routine screening for cognitive impairment is not recommended by the US Preventive Services Task Force or the American Academy of Neurology, cognitive difficulty observed or reported by individuals, their family members, or caregivers should prompt an evaluation. 68 , 69 There is no gold standard measure of cognitive impairment that has been universally validated for individuals with rheumatic diseases. 70 , 71 The utility of screening tools used in the general population for cognitive impairment in individuals with rheumatic diseases remains uncertain, particularly in young and middle-age groups (younger than 65 years) where the observed risk of cognitive impairment is disproportionally larger than expected due to the rheumatic disease itself. 72 , 73 Recent reports suggest suboptimal performance of the Montreal Cognitive Assessment in individuals with systemic lupus erythematosus who are age 18–65 years. 74 The need for tailoring cognitive testing tools to rheumatic diseases could differ by age group. Studies evaluating the relative risk of cognitive impairment and performance of tools for cognitive assessment in older adults with rheumatic diseases are absent. It can be hypothesised that, similar to the diminishing relative effect of rheumatic diseases on cardiovascular disease in older age groups, the relative effect of rheumatic diseases on cognition is smaller in individuals older than 65 years. 75 However, this hypothesis has not been formally tested. The American College of Rheumatology Neurocognitive Battery for the Assessment of Cognitive Dysfunction is essentially the only tool that has been validated against more traditional and longer comprehensive neuropsychological battery testing and is specific to systemic lupus erythematosus. 76 , 77 The process of evaluating cognitive impairment includes several steps: history taking from the individual or caregiver, using screening tools (eg, Mini-Cog, Mini-Mental State Examination, or the Montreal Cognitive Assessment), 78 performing extended mental status examination by a battery of tests for cognitive and behavioural concerns, and formal neuropsychological testing across the four main cognitive domains: memory, executive function, language and visuospatial skills. 68 We recognise that rheumatologists are not trained to conduct all levels of this rigorous testing and when concerns for cognitive impairment arise in individuals with rheumatic diseases at a rheumatology visit, patients should be referred for a comprehensive evaluation by a behavioural neurologist, neuropsychologist, or geriatric specialist ( figure 1 ). While systemic inflammation and autoimmunity in individuals with rheumatic diseases can be an important driver of cognitive impairment, other potential contributors and differential diagnosis should also be considered during the evaluation process for cognitive and memory concerns. Other reasons for neurocognitive impairment that can mimic dementia include delirium, cerebrovascular accidents, effects of medications, depression, and pain. Depression is twice as common in individuals with rheumatic diseases as in the general population 79 , 80 and is associated with cognitive impairment in up to 94% of individuals during depressive episodes. 81 A multidisciplinary review of a cognitively impaired individual with rheumatic diseases (eg, including specialists from rheumatology, neurology, psychiatry, cardiology, and geriatrics) can help avoid misclassification of cognitive impairment and identify the most likely underlying causes ( figure 1 ). 82 Glucocorticoid use is common in individuals with rheumatic diseases and has been associated with neuropsychiatric concerns in a dose-dependent manner, including delirium, depression, anxiety, insomnia, dementia-like cognitive changes, and impaired memory and concentration. 83 These problems generally resolve with discontinuation of glucocorticoids. As cognitive impairment can complicate rheumatologic diagnosis and management (ie, obtaining disease history, evaluation of pain, and functional ability), early recognition and individually tailored treatment of cognitive impairment can also benefit the outcome of rheumatic diseases (refer to the cognition Series paper). 48 Mobility In the context of the geriatric 5Ms, mobility encompasses the ability to move independently and safely, functional status, activities of daily living, balance and gait, fall prevention and fracture mitigation, and consideration of body composition factors. Mobility allows for the patient to maintain independence in everyday life and is particularly important for older adults and is an essential part of what matters most. Mobility restriction not only reduces overall quality of life but is an important risk factor for falls and fractures. One-third of adults age 65 years and older have had a fall and risk factors include previous falls, balance problems, potentially inappropriate medications use (eg, sedatives and anticholinergics), syndromes associated with dizziness and visual impairment, and impaired muscle function. 84 Falls can have serious health consequences and the mortality rate in the first year after a femoral neck fracture is over 20%. One in five individuals who sustain a femoral neck fracture require permanent nursing home care because they can no longer adequately care for themselves. 85 , 86 Counselling on fall prevention and optimising muscle strength and endurance are crucial components of managing mobility in older adults with rheumatic diseases, especially if taking glucocorticoids. 84 , 86 , 87 Several studies in older adults have shown that decreased muscle function is associated with increased fracture risk, reduced daily function, loss of independence, and mortality. 49 , 50 , 88 These data support the use of parameters such as walking speed, timed-up-and-go test, and handgrip strength as important signs in routine clinical practice. 49 , 88 However, in many specialties including rheumatology, the implementation of these performance-based measures have been limited. The observation that there is loss of muscle mass as part of the ageing process and that this loss can lead to functional limitations, led Irvin Rosenberg to coin the term sarcopenia. 89 Adults with rheumatic diseases are at particularly high risk of developing sarcopenia and functional limitations as several aspects of rheumatic diseases have a negative effect on muscle health. 90 , 91 Some rheumatic diseases, such as vasculitides and connective tissue diseases associated with myositis, cause muscle damage, weakness, and pain. Systemic inflammation, even without direct muscle involvement, can lead to muscle degradation, impaired self-renewal and repair, and functional deficits due to changes in mitochondrial function. 90 Additionally, joint pain and damage can lead to a decreased ability to produce a muscular force and power and result in muscle atrophy due to decreased physical activity. 21 , 22 As such, it is important for rheumatologists to be aware of the various measures for assessing muscle function and mass ( table ). The European Working Group on Sarcopenia in Older People suggests using the Strength, Assistance with walking, Rising from a chair, Climbing stairs, and Falls questionnaire as a screening tool, using muscle function tests like hand grip strength and chair-rise time to clinically assess sarcopenia, using lean mass assessment to establish sarcopenia, and using functional tests such as gait speed or Short Physical Performance Battery to classify sarcopenia severity. 49 With respect to prevention and management of sarcopenia, loss of mobility, and independence, non-pharmacological approaches including rehabilitation and physical activity and nutritional measures have been most successful, although few studies focus specifically on individuals with rheumatic diseases. 49 , 90 , 92 The European Alliance of Associations for Rheumatology (EULAR) published recommendations on exercise in 2018 93 and on lifestyle modifications in 2021 94 that include several recommendations to help prevent sarcopenia and loss of mobility. The literature suggests that most adults age 65 years and older with a diagnosis of a rheumatic disease do not meet the EULAR recommendations for physical activity, and those with lower activity levels also reported higher levels of pain, depression, and fatigue. 95 Individuals should adopt a combination of strength, balance, and endurance training, and protein rich diet to prevent sarcopenia. Current recommendations call for at least 1·0 g/kg, more likely 1·2–1·5 g/kg, (maybe even 2·0 g/kg in some instances) bodyweight protein per day (distributed over three meals) for people older than 65 years. A vitamin D3 deficiency should be compensated. Beneficial effects of other nutritional factors including omega-3 fatty acids, antioxidants, and pharmacological interventions on muscle mass and function have been shown, but data are scarce especially in older adults. 49 , 92 – 94 Ultimately the management of sarcopenia and mobility impairments requires, like all other geriatric syndromes, an interdisciplinary approach ( figure 1 ). Besides review of physical activity and nutrition, the assessment of fall risk, bone health, potential inappropriate medication, need for walking aids and other assistive devices, home safety, and several other factors are often necessary. What matters most A key component of health-care quality includes aligning health outcomes with individuals’ values, goals, and preferences. 96 As rheumatologists who care for patients with rheumatic diseases across their lifespan, it is important that shared decision making is conducted in the context of the individual’s preferences and goals, with the understanding that these can change over time. 97 The literature on the effect of goal-concordant care, especially in geriatrics, is growing. 98 Although it has not been specifically assessed in older individuals with rheumatic diseases, one strategy for enhancing care quality—particularly for those with multicomplexity, disabilities, or limited life expectancy—involves examining whether the unique health objectives of each patient (eg, managing symptoms and sustaining functional status) are being met. 99 , 100 Decision making in this context for older adults involves: communicating patient preferences and goals; estimating prognosis based on disease and non-disease factors (see palliative care Comment); 101 and understanding the role that cognitive, mobility, sensory impairments, and non-disease factors have on goal attainment. There are various approaches to asking patients to identify and communicate their goals and values ( figure 2 ). Basic principles involve using open-ended questions to allow older adults’ to freely express their thoughts and feelings without being constrained by a specific answer. For individuals who struggle to answer open-ended prompts, providing categories of core priorities that they could choose from can be helpful and informative. In addition to identifying existing goals, goal setting in the short-term or long-term can promote patient participation and adherence to treatment and can tailor the care plan to align with what matters most to them. It is also important to inquire about any fears they might have about ageing and explore end-of-life preferences, which could help identify areas where additional support or resources are needed to address the older adult’s priorities (see palliative care Comment). 101 Figure 2: Open in a new tab Approaches and questions to help older adults communicate about what matters most As yet, there is limited guidance focused on how rheumatologists can align the management of rheumatic disease care based on patient identified health priorities (ie, specific health outcome goals and care preferences). Other disciplines are starting to understand this process and are developing approaches for translating these priorities into shared clinical decisions and care. 31 , 53 , 102 Additional studies are needed to show feasibility and the effect on care for older adults with rheumatic diseases. Clinical approach As the population ages, it is important for rheumatologists to receive guidance on how best to deliver age-friendly care for older individuals with rheumatic diseases. The 5Ms framework equips rheumatologists with a strategic approach to swiftly pinpoint potential areas of concern, or red flags, in the health of older adults that might necessitate intervention. Once rheumatologists have identified one or multiple red flags, they can take initial steps themselves, such as simplifying DMARD medication regimens or referring a patient for physiotherapy to optimise muscle strength. However, when faced with more complex health issues, rheumatologists already recognise the constraints of clinical visit time and resources, along with limitations in their scope of expertise (particularly in areas such as cognitive evaluation within the geriatric 5Ms), and lean on colleagues and experts in other fields, such as geriatrics or neurology, for interdisciplinary care delivery ( figure 1 , table ). A core component of a visit to a geriatrician is the CGA. 7 It is known from the literature in other fields that implementing the CGA (or several components) improves outcomes in older adults. 103 The effectiveness of CGA seems particularly promising when integrated into a care programme with a proactive approach. For example, a Dutch study showed that implementing a nurse practitioner-led orthogeriatric care programme led to a significant decrease in 1-year mortality in patients with hip fracture (13·9% vs usual care, 34·0%). The nurse practitioner-led orthogeriatric care programme involved a preoperative CGA, medication review, inter-disciplinary ward rounds, discharge planning, nutritional therapy, physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and geriatric follow-up consultations. 104 Studies on the effect of the CGA in patients with rheumatic diseases are not available; however, studies suggests that geriatric syndromes are common in this patient population and the rheumatology community could adopt the geriatric 5Ms as a simplified approach to CGA. 11 Knowledge gaps, implementation considerations, and next steps There are a number of knowledge gaps and implementation challenges with regards to adopting the geriatric 5Ms in rheumatology ( table ). Perhaps the most pressing need to improve the care of older adults with rheumatic diseases is additional research to understand and implement the geriatric 5Ms effectively. Research across the translational spectrum (T0–T4, from basic science to proof-of-concept studies, clinical trials, medical practice, and population health) will be crucial for the rheumatology field to move our understanding of ageing forward. Importantly, older adults with rheumatic diseases should be enrolled in trials to contribute to the evidence base that is needed to inform clinical decision making 36 (see ageism Comment). 101 The 5Ts Framework (target population, team, time, tips to accommodate, and tools) was developed to support inclusion of older adults in research. 40 Other organisations have also encouraged justification of age across the lifespan ( table ). Standardisation of data capture across health-care systems and settings can facilitate implementation of the 5Ms. For example, multimorbidity, polypharmacy, and cognition are more frequently recognised as they can be identified readily electronic health records, although misclassification often causes issues. Additional work is needed to capture and standardise data elements for mobility, muscle function, what matters most, and the complex interplay of mentation (including depressive symptoms, anxiety, and social isolation) on the other 5Ms. Furthermore, studies need to establish how different health-care settings, policies, and forms of reimbursement in various countries affect care of older adults with rheumatic diseases. Research is needed to examine how these factors influence the implementation of the 5Ms principles and how effective assessment strategies such as the CGA are to address various geriatric syndromes in different countries and health-care settings. Some specific questions remain unanswered for each of the 5Ms ( table ). For multicomplexity, rheumatologists still need to understand what combination of comorbidities result in worse outcomes, how this influences medication use, and what matters most. This information can help health-care professionals prioritise what conditions to focus on first and the types of interdisciplinary teams needed to optimise outcomes that matter most to this population. For medication, we have provided several assessment tools, educational materials, and programmes that can inspire rheumatologists to consider ageing-related changes in safety and effectiveness of treatments that might affect outcomes. While we are not endorsing any particular screening tool for cognitive impairment in individuals with rheumatic diseases, we offer several options that might be considered in rheumatology clinics or in collaboration with an interdisciplinary team. For mobility, rheumatologists still need to learn how to best integrate assessment of body composition (eg, muscle, bone, and adiposity) into daily practice; we provide several validated assessment measures to consider. Ultimately, what matters most is what rheumatologists need to understand, including how to elicit the values, preferences, and priorities of the older adult in clinic, which can help prioritise the order that the 5Ms should be addressed. Conclusion This Series paper emphasises the relevance of the geriatric 5Ms for older adults with rheumatic diseases and highlights where further research is warranted to fully comprehend how to implement, assess, and manage the 5Ms. The older population with rheumatic disease is expanding rapidly, making it crucial to adopt age-friendly practices in rheumatology and understand key geriatric concepts to best care for ageing patients. Research on the influence and effect of the geriatric 5Ms on outcomes that matter most to older individuals will be crucial to develop quality measures and to inform policy changes. As a simple but powerful tool, the 5Ms underscore key principles of ageing, guiding and enhancing the care for complex older adults with rheumatic diseases. Search strategy and selection criteria. References for this Series paper were identified from searches of PubMed with the search terms: “Geriatric 5M”, “aging”, “geriatric principles”, “multicomplexity”, “multimorbidity”, “polypharmacy”, “deprescribing”, “mobility”, “falls”, “fractures”, “frailty”, “sarcopenia”, “loneliness”, “social isolation”, “cognition”, “cognitive impairment”, “dementia”, “values elicitation”, “what matters most”, “goals”, “rheum”, and in combination with specific rheumatic diseases, including “rheumatoid arthritis”, “psoriatic arthritis”, “lupus erythematosus, systemic”, “lupus”, “ankylosing spondylitis”, “sjogren’s syndrome”, vasculitis”, “osteoarthritis”, and “inflammation”, from database inception until Oct 31, 2023. Only papers published in English were reviewed. The fi