INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3845,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11442753','Risk factors for chronic kidney disease progression over 20 years for primary prevention in Japanese individuals at a preventive medicine research center: Focus on the influence of plasma glucose levels.','Minami K, Sakuma Y, Ogawa K, Takemura K, Takahashi H, Inoue T, Suzuki Y, Takahashi H, Shimura H, Sato Y, Watanabe S, Yoshida S, Ogino J, Hashimoto N.','2024','checkups tangri dkds dcct edic ukpds kumamoto vadt finerenone asahi asahi checkups echography hepatorenal brightness hepatologists nonsmokers arteriosclerotic checkup hyperferritinemia hypoadiponectinemia decliners decliner checkups hyperfiltration checkup asahi fortaleza nagler tangri elsayed aleppo aroda s191 s202 s127 genuth lachin parfrey sarnak s112 s119 hisashige kumamoto oellgaard gæde rossing microalbuminuria beigi craven banerji duckworth abraira ueki sasako okazaki gæde oellgaard ibsen steno bakris finerenone vart vaduganathan jongs albuminuric matsushita astor hanai babazono takagi bigotte leitao sprint kiriyama zucatti wayerbacher sakuma iwai hyperferritinemia yoshimoto sakuma moriya sone kashiwabara hirakawa decliner moriconi sacchetta chiriaco hyperfiltration cassano pelaia armentaro shafqat sabbah seva monico antunes hkm bakris aask ehteshami sarnak finerenone sathish khunti kmv rossing caramori jcn','ABSTRACT Aims/Introduction Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a very important issue globally because of the risk of its progressing to end‐stage renal disease. We aimed to identify factors contributing to long‐term estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) decline to determine an early diagnosis and prevent CKD progression. Materials and Methods From January 2003 to December 2006, 5,507 individuals underwent health checkups at our hospital''s Preventive Medicine Research Center. We ultimately enrolled 2,175 individuals. The eGFR was ≥60 mL/min/1.73 m 2 at the start of observation period, which was 20 years. The event onset time was the day that the eGFR became <30 mL/min during the 20‐year period. Baseline risk factors – in particular, the effect of plasma glucose levels on the eGFR – were extracted and evaluated by using Fine and Gray analysis. Results During the 20‐year observation, the hazard ratio (HR) of CKD progression was examined. A fasting plasma glucose (FPG) level ≥105 mg/dL was significantly associated with the risk of CKD progressing to an eGFR <30 mL/min. This trend was similar in the slope of eGFR. An FPG ≥105 mg/dL or an glycated hemoglobin level ≥6.5% was useful for intervening in CKD progression. Multivariate analysis showed that independent risk factors were an FPG level ≥105 mg/dL (HR 1.9; P < 0.001), age ≥60 years (HR 3.86; P < 0.001), obesity (HR 1.61; P < 0.01) and urinary protein (HR 1.55; P < 0.01). Conclusions For early intervention against a reduction in the eGFR, detecting mild increases in FPG ≥105 mg/dL in patients with CKD with or without diabetes is useful. Keywords: Chronic kidney disease progression, Estimated glomerular filtration rate, Plasma glucose For early intervention against a reduction in the estimated glomerular filtration rate, detecting mild increases in fasting plasma glucose ≥105 mg/dL in patients with chronic kidney disease with or without diabetes is useful. We would like to propose that a fasting plasma glucose level ≥105 mg/dL is an independent risk factor for the progression of chronic kidney disease, even in patients without diabetes. There are cases where estimated glomerular filtration rate levels decrease over a 20‐year period, even if they were maintained, and the elucidation of these factors will remain an issue in the future. INTRODUCTION Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is recognized as a very important issue globally because of the risk of its progressing to end‐stage renal disease, worsening cardiovascular events and increasing all‐cause mortality 1 . Diabetes is the leading cause of CKD globally, and the presence of diabetes is a strong risk factor for the progression of CKD to chronic kidney failure requiring replacement therapy 2 . Several models have been proposed with regard to risk factors for the development of CKD. Tangri et al . 2 first proposed eight items, which initially included age, sex, estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) and urinary albumin‐creatinine ratio, and later included serum calcium levels, phosphorus, bicarbonate and albumin levels. Diabetic kidney disease (DKD) is diagnosed in patients with diabetes when high plasma glucose levels are clinically the main cause of CKD. Among DKDs, the classic form of diabetic nephropathy involves persistent albuminuria, decreased eGFR, a history of diabetes for >10 years and retinopathy. However, for type 2 diabetes, the time of onset is often unclear, and DKD might already exist at the time of diagnosis. The American Diabetes Association guidelines use the term “diabetic kidney disease,” and the Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) guidelines use the terms “diabetes and chronic kidney disease” 3 , 4 . Among clinical trials for the prevention of microangiopathy, including nephropathy in diabetes patients, the DCCT/EDIC trial 5 , 6 focused on type 1 diabetes, and the UKPDS 7 , Kumamoto Study 8 , ADVANCE 9 , ACCORD 10 and VADT 11 studies focused on type 2 diabetes. These studies showed that strict blood glucose control can basically suppress the progression of microangiopathy, including in DKD. In addition, it has been reported that the progression of nephropathy can be prevented by multifactorial intervention of diet and exercise, plasma glucose, blood pressure, dyslipidemia control 12 , 13 , and ACE inhibitors, and angiotensin II receptor blockers suppress the increase in urinary albumin and the progression to renal failure. In recent years, the effects of sodium–glucose transport protein 2 inhibitors and glucagon‐like peptide‐1 analog preparations in DKD and CKD have also been reported. The aldosterone receptor blocker, finerenone, also suppresses nephropathy 14 , 15 . As mentioned previously, the progress in therapeutic medicine for CKD has been remarkable. However, not many clinical reports have focused on the risk factors for the onset of CKD, particularly plasma glucose levels, and the long‐term prevention of CKD onset. We believe that identifying risk factors before the onset of CKD, selecting patients and intervention by improving lifestyle behaviors are very important, given that disease progression cannot be completely suppressed, even with medical therapy. Furthermore, a belief is that the factors associated with the onset of CKD are largely influenced by the lifestyle behaviors of the country, as well as genetic factors. Analyzing risk factors over a long period would be valuable. For this reason, the Preventive Medicine Research Center of Asahi General Hospital (Asahi, Chiba, Japan) investigated the factors that lead to progression of CKD exacerbation, and clarified the prognostic factors by comparing patients who did and did not progress to an eGFR ≤30 mL/min over 20 years. MATERIALS AND METHODS Of the 5,507 participants who visited our hospital''s Preventive Medicine Research Center from January 2003 to December 2006 for health checkups, we excluded 485 patients with eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m 2 and 2,847 participants with a follow‐up period <15 years. In total, 2,175 participants were recruited. By using the Fine and Gray model, which takes into account death occurring during an observation period, the day that eGFR became <30 mL/min during the 20‐year period was used as an event to calculate the hazard ratios of various baseline factors that might be risk factors for eGFR falling below 30 mL/min during the observation period (Figure 1 ). We examined the receiver operating characteristic curve of the risk of eGFR <30 mL/min after 20 years, based on fasting plasma glucose (FPG). The amount of change per year was calculated from the baseline eGFR value and the eGFR value from 15 to 20 years. The average value of the slope for each concentration group was calculated and compared by using Student''s t ‐test with the FPG <96 mg/dL group and an glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) level <5.5%. We examined the hazard ratio for eGFR <30 mL/min and the slope of eGFR decrease, based on FPG levels. Furthermore, we examined the effect of FPG levels on the progression of CKD in individuals whose basal eGFR was >95 mL/min by using the Fine and Gray model. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flow diagram of the selection of participants at baseline. eGFR, estimated glomerular filtration rate. Fatty liver was diagnosed with abdominal ultrasound echography. Four criteria used for this diagnosis – hepatorenal echo contrast, liver brightness, deep attenuation and vascular blurring – were evaluated by hepatologists. Diabetes mellitus was diagnosed, based on an FPG level ≥126 mg/dL and HbA1c ≥6.5% (46.57 mmol/mol), or a self‐reported diagnosis by a physician, and/or the patient was taking insulin or an oral antidiabetic agent. Hypertension was diagnosed based on a systolic blood pressure ≥130 mmHg or diastolic blood pressure ≥80 mmHg. Obesity was defined as a body mass index ≥25 kg/m 2 , based on Japanese standard categories. Dyslipidemia was diagnosed, based on low‐density lipoprotein cholesterol ≥140 mg/dL, high‐density lipoprotein cholesterol <40 mg/dL or fasting triglyceride ≥150 mg/dL. Current and past smokers were defined as “smokers,” and individuals who had never smoked were defined as “nonsmokers.” The eGFR for men and women was calculated as follows: Men : eGFR mL / min / 1.73 m 2 = 194 × serum creatinine mg / dL − 1.094 × age (years) − 0.287 Women : eGFR mL / min / 1.73 m 2 = 194 × serum creatinine mg / dL − 1.094 × age (years) − 0.287 × 0.739 Statistical analysis We used the Fine and Gray model to examine the effects of the FPG level on the decline of eGFR to <30 mL/min, and the effects of FPG and HbA1c on the slope of the eGFR/year. Factors were extracted and multivariate analysis was carried out to evaluate the independence of each factor. In multivariate analysis, factors were selected by using the stepwise method. The effect of FPG levels on the progression of CKD in individuals whose basal eGFR was >95 mL/min was investigated by using the Fine and Gray model. RESULTS Figure 1 shows the flow diagram of the present study. A total of 2,175 participants were finally recruited for the analyses. The baseline characteristics are shown in Table 1 . Univariate analysis of eGFR <30 mL/min after 20 years, based on the baseline eGFR values, showed that significant factors were an age ≥60 years ( P < 0.001); male sex ( P < 0.05); obesity ( P < 0.001); eGFR ( P < 0.05); FPG level ( P < 0.001); HbA1c level ( P < 0.001); and the presence of diabetes mellitus ( P < 0.001), dyslipidemia ( P < 0.05), hypertension ( P < 0.05), fatty liver ( P < 0.05), kidney cysts ( P < 0.05) and urinary protein ( P < 0.01). Table 1. Baseline characteristics of participants, based on estimated glomerular filtration rate levels over 20 years Variable Overall eGFR ≥30 eGFR <30 P ‐value ( n = 2,175) ( n = 2016) ( n = 159) Age (years) 51.99 ± 8.90 51.56 ± 8.64 57.54 ± 10.32 <0.001 Age ≥60 years 419 (19.3%) 350 (17.4%) 69 (43.4%) <0.001 Men 1,442 (66.3%) 1,322 (65.6%) 120 (75.5%) 0.011 Obesity 718 (33.0%) 640 (31.7%) 78 (49.1%) <0.001 eGFR (mL/min/1.73 m 2 ) 80.10 ± 12.04 80.28 ± 11.74 77.87 ± 15.20 0.015 FPG (mg/dL) 100.67 ± 22.33 99.49 ± 19.26 115.72 ± 43.43 <0.001 HbA1c (%) 5.84 ± 0.82 5.79 ± 0.72 6.44 ± 1.50 <0.001 Diabetes mellitus 171 (7.9%) 131 (6.5%) 40 (25.2%) <0.001 Dyslipidemia 1,048 (48.2%) 959 (47.6%) 89 (56.0%) 0.048 Hypertension 1,254 (57.7%) 1,148 (56.9%) 106 (66.7%) 0.019 Smoking 666 (30.6%) 613 (30.4%) 53 (33.3%) 0.475 Uric acid (mg/dL) 5.35 ± 1.38 5.33 ± 1.37 5.51 ± 1.44 0.131 Fatty liver 716 (33.0%) 651 (32.3%) 65 (40.9%) 0.035 Urinary stone 42 (1.9%) 39 (1.9%) 3 (1.9%) 1.000 Kidney cysts 377 (17.3%) 340 (16.9%) 37 (23.3%) 0.049 Urinary protein 475 (21.9%) 424 (21.1%) 51 (32.1%) 0.002 Open in a new tab Data are presented as the mean ± the standard deviation or as the number (%), unless otherwise indicated. eGFR, estimated glomerular filtration rate; FPG, fasting plasma glucose; HbA1c, glycated hemoglobin. Focusing on the fact that FPG has a strong correlation with the risk of developing to eGFR <30 mL/min in long‐term observation, we examined the receiver operating characteristic curve for FPG level with eGFR <30 mL/min after 20 years. The FPG level with the best sensitivity and specificity was calculated. An FPG level of 105 mg/dL had the greatest risk, based on sensitivity and specificity. The area under the curve was 0.624 (95% confidence interval 0.575–0.674; specificity 0.798, sensitivity 0.415; 100% sensitivity 79 mg/dL; Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Receiver operating characteristic curve for fasting plasma glucose level with estimated glomerular filtration rate <30 mL/min after 20 years period. The area under the curve (AUC) was 0.624 (95% confidence interval 0.575–0.674, sensitivity 0.798, specificity 0.415) at fasting plasma glucose, and this was maximal. Kaplan–Meier analysis of CKD progression (i.e., eGFR <30 mL/min), using the Fine and Gray model was carried out. The hazard ratio (HR) for the FPG 105–125 mg/dL group ( P < 0.05), 126–145 mg/dL group ( P < 0.01) and >145 mg/dL group ( P < 0.01) was increased for the progression of a significant decrease in the eGFR to <30 mL/min, compared with the FPG <96 mg/dL group (Figure 3a ). We compared the FPG levels of 96–104, 105–125, 126–145 and >145 mg/dL with the FPG level of <96 mg/dL; the HRs for risk of an eGFR <30 mL/min were 1.05 (0.70–1.60, P = 0.81), 1.74 (1.12–2.68, P = 0.013), 5.04 (2.83–8.97, P < 0.001) and 5.42 (3.25–9.05, P < 0.001), respectively (Figure 3b ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab (a) Kaplan–Meier analysis of chronic kidney disease progression (i.e., estimated glomerular filtration rate <30 mL/min), using the Fine and Gray model. (b) Hazard ratio for estimated glomerular filtration rate <30 mL/min, divided by the levels of fasting plasma glucose (FPG). HR, hazard ratio. In addition, the eGFR of 32 participants (9.3%) with baseline FPG of 105–125 mg/dL progressed to an eGFR <30 min/dL during the 20‐year follow‐up period, and of the 159 who ultimately had eGFR <30 mL/min, 32 (20.1%) had FPG levels of 105–125 mg/dL (data not shown). The change in eGFR values per year was −0.73 ± 0.78 mL/min in the FPG <96 mg/dL group, −0.76 ± 0.78 mL/min in the FPG 96–104 mg/dL group and −0.81 ± 0.97 mL/min in the FPG 105–125 mg/dL group. A significant difference existed at −1.43 ± 1.38 mL/min in the FPG 126–145 mg/dL group, and at −1.77 ± 1.74 mL/min in the FPG >145 mg/dL group, compared with the other groups ( P < 0.01; Figure 4a ). Figure 4. Open in a new tab (a) The effects of the fasting plasma glucose (FPG) levels on the slope of the estimated glomerular filtration rate. (b) The effects of glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) levels on the slope of the estimated glomerular filtration rate. These values were significantly different in the >6.4% group, compared with the other groups. Values are expressed as the mean ± standard deviation. We also analyzed the slopes by baseline HbA1c levels. The slopes were −0.72 ± 0.73 mL/min for <5.5%, −0.74 ± 0.78 mL/min for 5.5–5.9%, −0.82 ± 0.94 mL/min for 6.0–6.4% and −1.28 ± 1.45 mL/min for >6.4%, respectively. A significant difference existed in the HbA1c >6.4% group, compared with the other groups (Figure 4b ). We examined the background levels of the eGFR in cases in which the eGFR decreased to <30 mL/min. We examined the effects of the FPG level on the progression of CKD in individuals with a baseline eGFR >95 mL/min ( n = 323). Although the background levels were significantly lower than that of the group that maintained an eGFR ( P < 0.05; 80.10 ± 12.04 mL/min vs 80.28 ± 11.47 mL/min, P < 0.05; Table 1 ), FPG levels in individuals with a basal eGFR >95 mL/min also had a significantly different effect on the progression of CKD (Figure 5 ). Figure 5. Open in a new tab The effects of the fasting plasma glucose (FPG) level on the progression of chronic kidney disease in individuals with a baseline estimated glomerular filtration rate >95 mL/min ( n = 323). In the Fine and Gray model, in the univariate analysis, the risk of developing eGFR <30 mL/min was significantly increased by the age of ≥60 years (HR 3.55), male sex (HR 1.57), obesity (i.e., body mass index ≥25; HR 2.06), hypertension (HR 1.50), FPG level ≥105 mg/dL (HR 2.61), uric acid level ≥6.5 mg/dL (HR 1.48), proteinuria (HR 1.72), kidney cyst (HR 1.47) and fatty liver (HR 1.48). Multivariate analysis showed that independent risk factors were an FPG level ≥105 mg/dL (HR 1.9, P < 0.001), age of ≥60 years (HR 3.86, P < 0.001), obesity (HR 1.61, P < 0.01) and urinary protein (HR 1.55, P < 0.01; Table 2 ). The highest hazard risk for the progression of CKD was an FPG level ≥105 mg/dL among these factors, except for an age ≥60 years. Table 2. Univariate and multivariate Fine and Gray analysis findings with regard to chronic kidney disease progression Factor Univariate analysis Multivariate analysis HR (95%CI) P ‐value aHR (95% CI) P ‐value aHR (95% CI) P ‐value Age ≥60 years 3.55 (2.59–4.85) <0.001 3.78 (2.75–5.20) <0.001 3.86 (2.81–5.32) <0.001 Male sex 1.57 (1.09–2.25) 0.015 1.09 (0.73–1.62) 0.690 Smoking 1.14 (0.82–1.58) 0.440 1.08 (0.75–1.54) 0.690 Obesity 2.06 (1.51–2.81) <0.001 1.56 (1.08–2.25) 0.017 1.61 (1.14–2.27) 0.007 Hypertension 1.5 (1.08–2.09) 0.016 1.12 (0.79–1.58) 0.540 Hyperlipidemia 1.37 (1.00–1.87) 0.051 1.09 (0.79–1.51) 0.590 FPG ≥105 mg/dL 2.61 (1.90–3.57) <0.001 1.86 (1.31–2.64) <0.001 1.91 (1.37–2.67) <0.001 Uric acid ≥6.5 mg/dL 1.48 (1.04–2.09) 0.029 1.37 (0.92–2.02) 0.120 1.40 (0.97–2.04) 0.076 Urinary protein 1.72 (1.23–2.40) 0.001 1.54 (1.11–2.16) 0.011 1.55 (1.11–2.15) 0.009 Kidney cysts 1.47 (1.02–2.12) 0.040 1.17 (0.80–1.70) 0.420 Urinary stone 1.07 (0.34–3.38) 0.910 0.88 (0.26–2.98) 0.830 Fatty liver 1.48 (1.08–2.04) 0.014 0.97 (0.68–1.38) 0.850 Open in a new tab aHR, adjusted hazard ratio; CI, confidence interval; CKD, chronic kidney disease; FPG, fasting plasma glucose. DISCUSSION A widely held belief is that a close relationship exists between CKD and life prognosis, and mortality from cardiovascular diseases in CKD is high 7 , 16 . Therefore, we believe that suppressing the progression of CKD is important for improving life prognosis. Indicators for the evaluation of CKD include eGFR and urinary protein. We believe that it is a little easier to intervene in the progression of CKD in an early stage than to intervene in other diseases or pathological conditions, such as potentially progressive arteriosclerotic disease. Therefore, we analyzed factors that worsened CKD over 20 years, based on medical checkup data, and we identified several factors. Reports showed that age, obesity 17 and the presence of urinary protein are factors in the progression of CKD. With regard to hyperglycemia in the current study, we found that, in addition to an FPG level of 126 mg/dL, which is an important value for the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus, an FPG level ≥105 mg/dL is an independent factor in the progression of CKD. We compared the effects of FPG and HbA1c on CKD progression. An HbA1c of 6.5% and FPG ≥126 mg/dL are diagnostic criteria for diabetes. The FPG level of 105 mg/dL is lower than the criterion for a diagnosis, which suggested a risk of deterioration in kidney function in these patients. An FPG level of 126 mg/dL for the diagnosis of diabetes has been established, based on the association with the development of retinopathy 18 , 19 . In the present study, glucose metabolism had a large influence on the progression of CKD. FPG and HbA1c levels are markers of glucose metabolism. However, the FPG level is widely measured in individuals with and without diabetes. Few papers exist on the association of FPG with CKD. Based on our literature search, several papers have reported on glycemic control indicators and CKD progression. Some reports showed that FPG levels are significantly more strongly correlated than are 2‐h plasma glucose levels. One report also showed that an insulin resistance index, the homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance, had the best correlation 20 , 21 . Although these studies involved several thousand people, the follow‐up period was only 2.3–3.3 years. However, one report 22 showed that FPG levels were not associated with CKD progression or the appearance of albuminuria; this study was also a 3.3‐year observation period of 9,000 people. The differences among these reports might be because of differences in the participants'' race or lifestyle, and because the observation period in each case was shorter than the period in the present study. We examined the annual decline in eGFR, and found a significant decrease in HbA1c ≥6.5% and FPG level ≥126 mg/dL, which suggested that the FPG level could be widely considered a risk factor for CKD, regardless of whether an individual did or did not have diabetes. Diabetic nephropathy is considered difficult to definitively diagnose in CKD, and DKD has been used globally in patients with CKD and diabetes. Impaired glucose tolerance and impaired fasting glucose levels are important targets for preventing the onset of diabetes, and these patients have been reported to have already progressed in terms of the development of cardiovascular disorders 23 , 24 , 25 . For CKD, a mild increase in FPG is predicted to progress in the same manner as a cardiovascular disease. The present study showed that interventions, such as lifestyle modification for obesity, will be useful in controlling the progression of CKD for a period, even if the FPG level is <126 mg/dL. The effects of hyperglycemia on CKD exacerbation might be related to various factors that contribute to the onset of diabetes. In previous studies 26 , 27 , we reported that hyperferritinemia in men and hypoadiponectinemia in women ultimately contributed to the onset of diabetes 15 years later. These factors might be partially responsible for the progression of CKD. Previous reports have focused on factors, other than plasma glucose levels, involved in the development of CKD. Although the number of cases was not substantial in one report, multivariate analysis carried out over an average of 15.6 months showed that hypercholesterolemia, hypoalbuminemia, lower basal eGFR, hypertension and so on were significant factors in the progression of CKD. However, among these factors, the presence of diabetes had a HR of 2.8 28 . Furthermore, in research on rapid eGFR decliners for type 2 diabetes, a study 29 carried out in Japan over 8 years among 1,407 patients reported that careful consideration is necessary, because rapid progression can occur, even in the presence of albuminuria and an eGFR of ≥120 mL/min. These reports suggest that factors exist that cannot be revealed simply with cross‐sectional analysis. In Japan, a paper reported on analyzed rapid decliner factors for 3.6 million health checkups over a period of approximately 1 year. According to this report, 21% of patients had diabetes, and factors that led to rapid deterioration of kidney function were high blood pressure, high plasma glucose levels and increased protein in the urine. In another report in Japan, 2,385 individuals with DKD were observed for 4 years. A total of 14% of the patients had rapid progression. A report 30 showed that older age, high eGFR value, high albuminuria and hypertension were associated with a lower eGFR. However, recent reports 31 have shown that the basal GFR and renal function deterioration might be unrelated to progression. Based on the present results, we also investigated the distribution of basal eGFR and cases of eGFR <30 mL/min after 20 years. We found that many cases of eGFR progression occurred among patients whose baseline eGFR was high. We believe that this result suggested that progression after 20 years cannot be predicted by using only the basal eGFR value, and that the analysis of various factors is important. In the present paper, we propose that selectively intervening at an early stage in patients who are likely to progress is important. This study, along with other previous reports, can be clinically important. We newly report that prediabetes with an FPG level of 105–125 mg/dL is a risk factor in CKD progression. The findings suggested that patients with this criterion have underlying insulin resistance; the fact that it is independent of body mass index suggested the presence of other factors, besides obesity. Thus, the presence of kidney‐related metabolic factors, cytokines and so on, owing to the presence of insulin resistance, might be involved in the progression of CKD. In recent research, the epigenome, which is a post‐translational modification, is responsible for the development of hyperglycemia and CKD; in addition, mildly elevated FPG might be involved in the exacerbation of CKD 32 , 33 , 34 . Investigators also report that oxidative stress is important for the development of CKD due to the increase of plasma glucose 35 . To date, many reports 36 indicate that obesity and insulin resistance are correlated with the progression of CKD. For example, the effects on the kidneys after gastric surgery are interesting in terms of the effects on the kidneys in improving obesity. According to reports, no evaluation has definitely determined whether the GFR improves because of the influence of preoperative glomerular hyperfiltration. However, some reports 37 indicate that improving obesity improves urine albumin, proteinuria and the progression to end‐stage renal disease. Of course, it has been reported that by bariatric surgery, the remission of diabetes and improvement of metabolic indicators for normal CKD can generally be expected. Unexpectedly, hypertension was not a significant risk factor for eGFR <30 mL/min in multivariate analysis in the present study. In this study, we believe that there might have been substantial evidence of medical therapy for CKD, especially antihypertensive medicine, accumulated over the 20‐year period, and these interventions might have been carried out 38 , 39 , 40 . Therefore, we believe that this was also one of the reasons why hypertension and dyslipidemia did not remain significant factors for CKD progression in the multivariate analysis, and our current study does not deny the importance of antihypertensive therapy in preventing the onset or progression of CKD. Recent advances in therapeutic medicine for diabetes have made possible the use of sodium–glucose transport protein 2 inhibitors and glucagon‐like peptide‐1 receptor agonists; each are useful for treating kidney disease 41 . During most of our observation period in the present study, we were unable to use these drugs, even if the patients had diabetes. More than 90% of the participants at baseline did not have diabetes, and sodium–glucose transport protein 2 inhibitors were released in Japan in 2014 and were approved for CKD from September 2022, so we believe that the effects of these medicines during this follow‐up period was small. Therefore, we believe that the effects of baseline FPG and HbA1c levels on CKD progression were followed up relatively correctly in the observation period in our study. A recent interesting meta‐analysis paper 42 reported that preventing diabetes by using conventional lifestyle interventions is difficult for patients with isolated impaired fasting glucose, which suggests that pharmacotherapy might be necessary. The present study also had limitations. The first limitation is that the baseline risk factors listed in our study were present 20 years earlier. Therefore, how the disease progressed during that time is unclear, and no information existed regarding lifestyle behaviors or medical treatments. The second limitation is that genetic background and family history, which have long been considered important, were not clarified. Recent advances in drug therapy to treat the progression of CKD, and the updated KDIGO 2022 treatment guidelines are expected to suppress the progression to end‐stage renal disease in the future 43 . However, given increasing medical costs, we believe that suppressing the progression to CKD through lifestyle changes and other interventions is extremely important in people with normal renal function who have many risk factors for the progression of CKD. In conclusion, diabetes, age, obesity and the presence of proteinuria were underlying factors for the progression to stage 4 CKD over a 20‐year period in the health checkup of participants without CKD. These risk factors have been previously reported. At this time, we would propose that an FPG level ≥105 mg/dL is an independent risk factor for the progression of CKD, even in patients without diabetes. This is one of the factors in intervening as a risk of CKD progression. There are cases where eGFR levels decrease over a 20‐year period, even if they were maintained, and the elucidation of these factors will remain an issue in the future. FUNDING This work was supported by grants from the Japan Association for Diabetes Education and Care (grant no.: 2022‐FND‐003). DISCLOSURE All authors declare no conflict of interest. Approval of the research protocol: The protocol for this research project has been approved by the ethics committee of Asahi General Hospital approved the study on 15 November 2022 (approval no. 2022111505), and we confirm the all procedures followed were in accordance with the ethical standards of the responsible committee on human experimentation (institutional and national) and with the Declaration of Helsinki (as revised in Fortaleza, Brazil, October 2013). Informed consent: We applied the opt‐out method to obtain informed consent for this study. Approval data of registry and the registration no. of this study/trial: N/A. We did not register in a publicly database because this study was retrospective study. Animal studies: N/A. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thank all staff in the Clinical Research Support Center. We thank Dr Barry J Goldstein for helpful comments for our paper. References 1. Webster AC, Nagler EV, Morton RL, et al . Chronic kidney disease. Lancet 2017; 389: 1238–1252. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Tangri N, Stevens LA, Griffith J, et al . A predictive model for progression of chronic kidney disease to kidney failure. JAMA 2011; 305: 1553–1559. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. ElSayed NA, Aleppo G, Aroda VR, et al . 11. Chronic kidney disease and risk management: standards of care in diabetes‐2023. Diabetes Care 2023; 46: S191–s202. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. KDIGO . Clinical practice guideline for diabetes management in chronic kidney disease. Kidney Int 2022; 2022: S1–s127. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Nathan DM, Genuth S, Lachin J, et al . The effect of intensive treatment of diabetes on the development and progression of long‐term complications in insulin‐dependent diabetes mellitus. N Engl J Med 1993; 329: 977–986. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. de Boer IH, Sun W, Cleary PA, et al . Intensive diabetes therapy and glomerular filtration rate in type 1 diabetes. N Engl J Med 2011; 365: 2366–2376. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Foley RN, Parfrey PS, Sarnak MJ. Clinical epidemiology of cardiovascular disease in chronic renal disease. Am J Kidney Dis 1998; 32: S112–S119. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Wake N, Hisashige A, Katayama T, et al . Cost‐effectiveness of intensive insulin therapy for type 2 diabetes: A 10‐year follow‐up of the Kumamoto study. Diabetes Res Clin Pract 2000; 48: 201–210. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Oellgaard J, Gæde P, Rossing P, et al . Intensified multifactor