cted, the project and the role they could play were explained, and all were provided with information pamphlets and brochures. Workshops were conducted with the local authority health inspectorate to explain the project and to update their knowledge of pesticide poisoning, and its prevention, detection and reporting. Existing reporting forms were supplemented with additional questions. Follow‐up of 1 year for cases of deliberate ingestion, 5 months for the other events. Outcomes Through a passive notification system enhanced by the awareness programme: Number of notifications of pesticide poisonings (events and cases) Annual mean rate of notification of cases (per 100,000 population) Notes Supported by a grant from the Department of Health of the Primary Care Research Fund Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) High risk Not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) High risk Not done 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests High risk Not appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Unclear risk Number of participants (i.e. physicians) was not reported 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) High risk The participating area was compared to different areas 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk The study was conducted from August 1994 to July 1995 23. Randomisation High risk No randomisation 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Not randomised, not applicable 25. Adjustment for confounding High risk No adjustment for confounders 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk Participants lost to follow‐up were not reported Open in a new tab Brissette 2006a . Methods RCT (with 2 intervention arms and 1 control arm) Aim: evaluation of the effectiveness of using different appeals to promote complete and timely reporting to the New York State Occupational Lung Disease Registry (NYS OLDR) Participants Population description: 368 physicians identified through hospital medical records as having provided care to patients with lung conditions reportable to the NYS OLDR who had not reported these cases (17 physicians excluded because of inappropriate address and 1 due to retirement) Location: New York state (USA) Inclusion criteria: physicians who had provided care to patients with lung conditions reportable to the NYS OLDR and had not previously reported any cases to the OLDR Recruitment: from September 2003 to the first week of March 2004, letters and materials were mailed to 368 physicians identified through hospital medical records as having provided care to patients (n = 5424) with lung conditions reportable to the NYS OLDR who had not reported these cases Demographics: not reported Interventions Educational materials: messages (1 message per not‐reported case; 44 physicians, equally distributed among the 3 groups, received more than 1 message) 3 different message groups (see also Brissette 2006b ): Group 1: ''obligation message group'' (intervention) received messages and materials emphasizing the legal obligation to report to the OLDR (n = 105) Group 2: ''benefit message group'' (control) received messages and materials emphasizing the benefits of reporting to the OLDR (n = 124, divided over the 2 intervention arms to prevent double counting of study participants in the meta‐analysis) Group 3: ''obligation plus benefit message group'' (see Brissette 2006b ) Obligation message group Letter to physician emphasizing legal requirement to report to the OLDR Excerpt of public health law mandating the reporting of occupational lung disease to New York State Department of Health 1‐page flyer with bold 24‐point font reading "Public health law requires that physicians report patients with occupational lung disease to the Occupational Lung Disease Registry. To comply with this requirement, please report this and future patients to the Registry by completing and returning the accompanying form" Brochure describing how to report to the OLDR and listing the OLDR reportable conditions Reporting form with patient information from the previous hospital report Additional reporting form Benefit message group Letter to physician emphasizing benefits of reporting to the OLDR Excerpt of public health law mandating the reporting of occupational lung diseases to New York State Department of Health 1‐page flyer with bold 24‐point font reading "Public health law requires that physicians report patients with occupational lung disease to the Occupational Lung Disease Registry. To comply with this requirement, please report this and future patients to the registry by completing and returning the accompanying form" Brochure describing New York State Occupational Health Clinic Network Reporting form with patient information from the previous hospital report Additional reporting form Follow‐up of 10 days after the message has been received Outcomes Response rate: number (or proportion) of physicians in the 3 message groups who returned an OLDR reporting form by fax or mail Other outcomes studied were timeliness and completeness of reporting. Notes In RevMan analyses we divided the control group evenly over the 2 study arms with 27 events and 62 participants each. The activities were funded by the National institute of Occupational Safety and Health under grant 5U 010H0073 08. Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk Individuals who were unaware of the message groups to which physicians were assigned tracked the dependent measures 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests High risk Only Chi 2 test was used 19. Compliance Low risk Re