 1: ''obligation message group'' (see Brissette 2006a ) Group 2: ''benefit message group'' (control) received messages and materials emphasizing the benefits of reporting to the OLDR (n = 124, divided over the 2 intervention arms to prevent double counting of study participants in the meta‐analysis) Group 3: ''obligation plus benefit message group'' (intervention) received messages and materials conveying both the obligation to report plus the benefits of reporting (n = 121) Benefit message group Letter to physician emphasizing benefits of reporting to the OLDR Excerpt of public health law mandating the reporting of occupational lung diseases to New York State Department of Health 1‐page flyer with bold 24‐point font reading "Public health law requires that physicians report patients with occupational lung disease to the Occupational Lung Disease Registry. To comply with this requirement, please report this and future patients to the registry by completing and returning the accompanying form" Brochure describing New York State Occupational Health Clinic Network Reporting form with patient information from the previous hospital report Additional reporting form Obligation plus benefit message group Letter to physician emphasizing legal requirement to report and benefits of reporting to the OLDR Excerpt of public health law mandating the reporting of occupational lung diseases to New York State Department of Health 1‐page flyer with bold 24‐point font reading "To assist the state’s prevention and surveillance efforts, Public Health Law requires physicians to report patients with occupational lung disease to the occupational lung disease registry. To report this and future patients, please complete and return the accompanying form" Brochure describing how to report to the OLDR and listing the OLDR reportable conditions Brochure describing New York State Occupational Health Clinic Network Reporting form with patient information from the previous hospital report Additional reporting form Follow‐up of 10 days after the message has been received Outcomes Response rate: number (or proportion) of physicians in the 2 message groups who returned an OLDR reporting form by fax or mail Other outcomes studied were timeliness and completeness of reporting. Notes In RevMan analyses we divided the control group evenly over the 2 study arms, with 27 events and 62 participants each. The activities were funded by the National Institute of Occupational Safety and Health under grant 5U 010H0073 08. Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk Individuals who were unaware of the message groups to which physicians were assigned tracked the dependent measures 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests High risk Only Chi 2 test was used 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in different intervention groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in different intervention groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation Low risk Participants were randomly assigned to the intervention groups. The method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding High risk No adjustment for confounders 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Gelberg 2011 . Methods Classified as a CBA study using as control group states other than New York (data obtained via personal communication) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of an intervention to increase the reporting of occupational lung disorders in New York state to the already existing Occupational Lung Disease Registry (OLDR) Participants Population description: healthcare providers Location: New York state Inclusion criteria for healthcare providers: none Control: physicians working in New Jersey were considered as a reference group (data obtained via personal communication) Demographics: not reported Interventions Multifaceted intervention executed in 2003: 2 1‐page articles on diagnostic techniques for work‐related asthma and occupational diseases published in a variety of newsletters distributed to a broad range of medical‐care providers; letters emphasizing both legal and public health bases for reporting occupational lung diseases were sent to physicians who were likely to treat patients with occupational lung diseases and had not reported within the last year; requests via mail of specific information about patients with lung conditions reportable to the OLDR to physicians identified by hospital reports as having provided care to those patients; and multiple enhanced web pages on the New York State Department of Health website to improve access to information about the Bureau of Occupational Health (BOH) with links to the various BOH programs, including information aimed at increasing awareness of the OLDR and its reporting requirements and promoting the recognition of occupational lung diseases. Intervention executed in 2005: toolkit containing information on the OLDR, occupational health, and work‐related asthma was sent to physicians in the following practice areas: allergy and immunology, emergency medicine, family practice, internal medicine, preventive medicine, public health, occupational health, and environmental medicine Follow‐up: 1 year Outcomes Number of physicians reporting Other outcomes studied were new physicians initiating a report, number of reports received (received due to prompt, received without a prompt) Notes The original data reported by the authors did not allow us to classify this study as a CBA. We retrieved from the original study the data regarding the number of events of the intervention group (that is number of physicians reporting occupational diseases in New York state; n = 9 (before the intervention) and n = 294 (after the intervention)). We extracted from the U.S. Census Bureau, Statistical Abstract of the United States the data regarding the denominator of the intervention group (that is total number of physicians in New York state; n = 73,710 (before the intervention) and n = 74,924 (after the intervention)) ( U.S. Census Bureau ). We obtained from a personal communication the data regarding the number of events of the control group (that is number of physicians reporting occupational diseases in New Jersey; n = 6 (before the intervention) and n = 9 (after the intervention)). We extracted from the U.S. Census Bureau, Statistical Abstract of the United States the data regarding the denominator (that is total number of physicians in New Jersey, n = 26,183 (before the intervention) and n = 26,580 (after the intervention)) ( U.S. Census Bureau ). Since the intervention under evaluation took place in 2003, we considered 2002 and 2004 as before and after intervention period, respectively. This research was funded in part by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health Cooperative Agreement #5U01 OH07308. Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) High risk Not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) High risk Not done 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests High risk No statistical tests used 19. Compliance Unclear risk Data about compliance referred to a part of intervention only 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) High risk The participating area was compared to different areas 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in intervention and comparison groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation High risk No randomisation 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Not randomised, not applicable 25. Adjustment for confounding High risk No adjustment for confounders 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk Data about lost to follow‐up referred to a part of intervention only Open in a new tab Lenderink 2010a . Methods RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of supplying occupational physicians with targeted and stage‐matched information or with feedback on reporting occupational diseases to the national registry of the Netherlands Participants Population description: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population 1079 occupational physicians were divided into 3 groups (see also Lenderink 2010b , Lenderink 2010c , Lenderink 2010d , Lenderink 2010e ) based on previous reporting behaviour: ''precontemplators'' (not reporting any occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007); ''contemplators'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, but not after May 2007); and ''actioners'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, including the last six months of 2007). Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population Recruitment: selection from the database of the Dutch National Registry of Occupational Diseases Demographics: sex, employment status, working hours/week Interventions Intervention: precontemplators were randomly assigned to receive personalised information (n = 180, intervention group: personally addressed electronic newsletter on pros and cons of reporting occupational diseases) and general information (n = 206, control group: short electronic message) Follow‐up: outcomes were measured in 180 days before and 180 days after the intervention Outcomes Number of occupational physicians reporting Number of reported occupational diseases per physician (rate of reporting) Notes RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Of the 5 intervention arms, 1 had its own control arm ( Lenderink 2010c ). Of the other 4 intervention arms, 1 couple ( Lenderink 2010a and Lenderink 2010d ) had a unique control arm, and the other couple ( Lenderink 2010b and Lenderink 2010e ) had another, unique control arm (different from the other 2 control arms). Since in the meta‐analysis they did not belong to the same comparison, we did not divide the number of events and participants in the control group equally over the study arms. In this case, it was not necessary to prevent double counting of study participants Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk The number of notifications was obtained from the national register. The intervention group did not affect these numbers 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests Low risk Appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation Low risk Participants were randomly assigned to the intervention or control group. The method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding Low risk Distribution of confounders in the different intervention groups was described 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Lenderink 2010b . Methods RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of supplying occupational physicians with targeted and stage‐matched information or with feedback on reporting occupational diseases to the national registry of the Netherlands Participants Population description: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population 1079 occupational physicians were divided into 3 groups (see also Lenderink 2010a , Lenderink 2010c , Lenderink 2010d , Lenderink 2010e ) based on previous reporting behaviour: ''precontemplators'' (not reporting any occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007); ''contemplators'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, but not after May 2007); and ''actioners'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, including the last six months of 2007). Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population Recruitment: selection from the database of the Dutch National Registry of Occupational Diseases Demographics: sex, employment status, working hours/week Interventions Intervention: contemplators were randomly assigned to receive personalised information (n = 89, intervention group: personally addressed electronic newsletter on pros and cons of reporting occupational diseases) and general information (n = 94, control group: short electronic message) Follow‐up: outcomes were measured in 180 days before and 180 days after the intervention Outcomes Number of occupational physicians reporting Number of reported occupational diseases per physician (rate of reporting) Notes RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Of the 5 intervention arms, 1 had its own control arm ( Lenderink 2010c ). Of the other 4 intervention arms, 1 couple ( Lenderink 2010a and Lenderink 2010d ) had a unique control arm, and the other couple ( Lenderink 2010b and Lenderink 2010e ) had another, unique control arm (different from the other 2 control arms). Since in the meta‐analysis they did not belong to the same comparison, we did not divide the number of events and participants in the control group equally over the study arms. In this case, it was not necessary to prevent double counting of study participants Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk The number of notifications was obtained from the national register. The intervention group did not affect these numbers 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests Low risk Appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation Low risk Participants were randomly assigned to the intervention or control group. The method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding Low risk Distribution of confounders in different intervention groups was described 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Lenderink 2010c . Methods RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of supplying occupational physicians with targeted and stage‐matched information or with feedback on reporting occupational diseases to the national registry of the Netherlands Participants Population description: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population 1079 occupational physicians were divided into 3 groups (see also Lenderink 2010a , Lenderink 2010b , Lenderink 2010d , Lenderink 2010e ) based on previous reporting behaviour: ''precontemplators'' (not reporting any occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007); ''contemplators'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, but not after May 2007); and ''actioners'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007 up to 27 November 2007). Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population Recruitment: selection from the database of the Dutch National Registry of Occupational Diseases Demographics: sex, employment status, working hours/week Interventions Intervention: actioners, that is physicians who reported a notification in 2006 and 2007, including the last 6 months, were randomly assigned to intervention (n = 118) or control group (n = 120). Then, among the randomised physicians, only those who notified an occupational disease in the 6‐month period following 27 November 2007 received either a personalised feedback (that is intervention group) that consisted of extra information referring to the diagnosis notified or a standardised feedback (that is control group) that consisted of notification acceptance only Follow‐up: outcomes were measured in a 180‐day period before 27 November 2007 and in a 180‐day period after the intervention, corresponding to the date of their last notification Outcomes Number of reported occupational diseases per physician (rate of reporting) Notes RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Of the 5 intervention arms, 1 had its own control arm ( Lenderink 2010c ). Of the other 4 intervention arms, 1 couple ( Lenderink 2010a and Lenderink 2010d ) had a unique control arm, and the other couple ( Lenderink 2010b and Lenderink 2010e ) had another, unique control arm (different from the other 2 control arms). Since in the meta‐analysis they did not belong to the same comparison, we did not divide the number of events and participants in the control group equally over the study arms. In this case, it was not necessary to prevent double counting of study participants The numbers reported in the article for the actioners ( Lenderink 2010c ) were slightly different from those used in the review, which were obtained via personal communication from the authors (that is the number of occupational diseases reported was 343 out of 118 physicians in the intervention group and 407 out of 119 physicians in the control group). We finally agreed with the authors to use these numbers as were considered as being correct Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk The number of notifications was obtained from the national register. The intervention group did not affect these numbers 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests High risk No statistical tests used 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation High risk Participants (defined by the authors as ''actioners'') were randomly assigned to the intervention or control group; only those who notified an occupational disease in a specific time period received either a personalised or a standardised feedback. Consequently, the number of physicians randomised was larger than the number of those who underwent the intervention, resulting in a subset much different from that initially randomised. This led us to believe that the randomisation had not been properly conducted. Moreover, the method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding Low risk Distribution of confounders in different intervention groups was described 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Lenderink 2010d . Methods RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of supplying occupational physicians with targeted and stage‐matched information or with feedback on reporting occupational diseases to the national registry of the Netherlands Participants Population description: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population 1079 occupational physicians were divided into 3 groups (see also Lenderink 2010a , Lenderink 2010b , Lenderink 2010c , Lenderink 2010e ) based on previous reporting behaviour: ''precontemplators'' (not reporting any occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007); ''contemplators'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, but not after May 2007); and ''actioners'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, including the last six months of 2007). Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population Recruitment: selection from the database of the Dutch National Registry of Occupational Diseases Demographics: sex, employment status, working hours/week Interventions Intervention: precontemplators were randomly assigned to receive personalised information (n = 180, intervention group: personally addressed electronic newsletter on self efficacy enhancing information on how to report, where to find information, guidelines, offer to participate in a workshop on reporting occupational diseases) and general information (n = 206, control group: short electronic message) Follow‐up: outcomes were measured in 180 days before and 180 days after the intervention Outcomes Number of occupational physicians reporting Number of reported occupational diseases per physician (rate of reporting) Notes RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Of the 5 intervention arms, 1 had its own control arm ( Lenderink 2010c ). Of the other 4 intervention arms, 1 couple ( Lenderink 2010a and Lenderink 2010d ) had a unique control arm, and the other couple ( Lenderink 2010b and Lenderink 2010e ) had another, unique control arm (different from the other 2 control arms). Since in the meta‐analysis they did not belong to the same comparison, we did not divide the number of events and participants in the control group equally over the study arms. In this case, it was not necessary to prevent double counting of study participants Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk The number of notifications was obtained from the national register. The intervention group did not affect these numbers 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests Low risk Appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation Low risk Participants were randomly assigned to the intervention or control group. The method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding Low risk Distribution of confounders in different intervention groups was described 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Lenderink 2010e . Methods RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Aim: to assess the effectiveness of supplying occupational physicians with targeted and stage‐matched information or with feedback on reporting occupational diseases to the national registry of the Netherlands Participants Population description: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population 1079 occupational physicians were divided into 3 groups (see also Lenderink 2010a , Lenderink 2010b , Lenderink 2010c , Lenderink 2010d ) based on previous reporting behaviour: ''precontemplators'' (not reporting any occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007); ''contemplators'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, but not after May 2007); and ''actioners'' (reporting occupational diseases in 2006 and 2007, including the last six months of 2007). Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: occupational physicians who are registered to notify occupational diseases in the national registry and are assigned to a workforce population Recruitment: selection from the database of the Dutch National Registry of Occupational Diseases Demographics: sex, employment status, working hours/week Interventions Intervention: contemplators were randomly assigned to receive personalised information (n = 90, intervention group: personally addressed electronic newsletter on self efficacy enhancing information on how to report, where to find information, guidelines, offer to participate in a workshop on reporting occupational diseases) and general information (n = 94, control group: short electronic message) Follow‐up: outcomes were measured in 180 days before and 180 days after the intervention Outcomes Number of occupational physicians reporting Number of reported occupational diseases per physician (rate of reporting) Notes RCT (with 5 intervention arms and 3 different control arms) Of the 5 intervention arms, 1 had its own control arm ( Lenderink 2010c ). Of the other 4 intervention arms, 1 couple ( Lenderink 2010a and Lenderink 2010d ) had a unique control arm, and the other couple ( Lenderink 2010b and Lenderink 2010e ) had another, unique control arm (different from the other 2 control arms). Since in the meta‐analysis they did not belong to the same comparison, we did not divide the number of events and participants in the control group equally over the study arms. In this case, it was not necessary to prevent double counting of study participants Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) Unclear risk Probably not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) Low risk The number of notifications was obtained from the national register. The intervention group did not affect these numbers 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests Low risk Appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation Low risk Participants were randomly assigned to the intervention or control group. The method of randomisation was not mentioned 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Probably not done 25. Adjustment for confounding Low risk Distribution of confounders in different intervention groups was described 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Maizlish 1995 . Methods CBA Aim: to enhance quantity and quality of case reporting of acute work‐related pesticide illness Participants Population description: local healthcare providers Intervention: 102 physicians Control: 389 physicians Inclusion criteria: physicians committed to take part in the Sentinel Event Notification System for Occupational Risks and recruited in face‐to‐face meetings Location: Fresno County, California (USA) Recruitment: a survey was sent to 491 county physicians likely to treat occupational pesticide illness. 102 physicians were recruited in face‐to‐face‐meetings from survey respondents Demographics: not reported Interventions Educational materials: reporting physicians were given a manual covering reporting requirements and guidelines, report forms, and educational materials. The guidelines directed providers to report suspected or confirmed pesticide illness in 4 specific categories and 1 general category related to pesticide exposure Follow‐up of 3 years Outcomes Number of reported pesticide illness cases Notes ‐ Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) High risk Not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) High risk Not done 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No data dredging 17. Follow‐up Low risk Same time period 18. Statistical tests High risk No statistical tests used 19. Compliance Unclear risk Not reported 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation High risk No randomisation 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Not randomised, not applicable 25. Adjustment for confounding High risk No adjustment for confounders 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab Smits 2008 . Methods CBA Aim: to study the effectiveness of an active, multifaceted workshop aimed at improving the reporting of occupational diseases by occupational physicians Participants Population description: occupational physicians Intervention: occupational physicians from large Occupational Health Services in the Netherlands (n = 112) Control: occupational physicians selected from the database of the National Center of Occupational Diseases (n = 571) Location: the Netherlands Inclusion criteria: not reported Recruitment: not reported Demographics: not reported Interventions Intervention: 1‐day multifaceted workshop focusing on the reporting of 4 occupational diseases for which Dutch notifying guidelines were available Follow‐up: occupational‐disease reporting activity was measured 6 months before and 6 months after the intervention Outcomes Percentage of occupational physicians reporting occupational diseases Average number of reported occupational diseases per reporting physician Other outcomes: occupational physicians'' knowledge, self efficacy, and satisfaction were evaluated in the intervention group Notes ‐ Risk of bias Bias Authors'