icians in the Netherlands in the national registry (n = 1729) were the reference group Follow‐up: 2 years (the results were also compared to the 2 preceding years) Outcomes Median number of notifications per occupational physician Other outcomes studied were proportion of incorrect notifications and reported incidence of occupational diseases Notes ‐ Risk of bias Bias Authors'' judgement Support for judgement 14. Blinding (subjects) High risk Not done 15. Blinding (outcome assessors) High risk Not done 16. Retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses Low risk No retrospective unplanned subgroup analyses 17. Follow‐up Low risk The same follow‐up time for all participants 18. Statistical tests Low risk Appropriate statistical tests 19. Compliance Low risk Reliable compliance 20. Outcome measures Low risk Valid outcome measures were used 21. Selection bias (population) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited from the same population 22. Selection bias (time) Low risk Participants in the intervention and control groups were recruited over the same period of time 23. Randomisation High risk No randomisation 24. Allocation concealment Unclear risk Not randomised, not applicable 25. Adjustment for confounding High risk No adjustment for confounders 26. Incomplete outcome data Unclear risk The proportion of participants lost to follow‐up is not known Open in a new tab CBA: controlled before‐after study RCT: randomised controlled trial Characteristics of excluded studies [ordered by study ID] Study Reason for exclusion Ameille 2003 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational asthma; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Bawa 2005 Controlled before‐after study executed with the aim of evaluating the effectiveness of a training workshop to increase the number of reported infectious diseases (non‐occupational diseases) Cherry 2000 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational skin diseases; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Chu 2010 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning reporting of occupational diseases, based on hospital centers whose main tasks were consultation and management of occupational diseases and maintenance of their reporting system. Data personally provided by co‐authors of the article did not permit classification of the study as an interrupted time‐series Dano 1996 Descriptive study reporting data on the lack of improvement in occupational‐cancer reporting De Lamberterie 2002 Descriptive study reporting data of a university hospital surveillance system concerning occupational lung cancers; study provided information about aetiology Esterhuizen 2001 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational asthma; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Frazier 1994 Study described a new surveillance program concerning mandatory reporting of occupational health problems Goldberg 2006 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning mesothelioma; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Maizlish 1995a Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning carpal tunnel syndrome; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Orriols 2010 Descriptive study reporting data of a voluntary surveillance system concerning occupational respiratory diseases in three regions; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology. Data personally provided by the first author of the article did not permit classification of the study as an interrupted time‐series or a controlled before‐after study Provencher 1997 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational respiratory diseases; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Rapparini 2007 Study described the results of an Internet‐based project that had as its main objective disseminating knowledge about and discussing occupational infections among healthcare workers Reinisch 2001 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational asthma; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Rosenman 2003 It describes the results of a capture‐recapture analysis aimed at estimating the number of silicosis cases Seixas 1986 It describes the attempt to establish a reporting system of occupational diseases Spreeuwers 2012 Descriptive study reporting data of a voluntary surveillance system concerning reporting of occupational diseases, based on collaboration of all the 18 occupational health specialists of the island. The article provided only the data for the period after the intervention, and the authors were not able to personally provide the data for the years before the intervention. We were therefore unable to classify the study as an interrupted time‐series Squires 1998 Randomised controlled trial executed with the aim of evaluating the effectiveness of an intervention (letter, invitation to report via 24‐hour direct telephone line, educational materials) to increase the number of reported infectious diseases (varicella and pertussis) Tarlo 2002 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational asthma; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Ward 2008 Randomised controlled trial executed with the aim of evaluating the effectiveness of an Internet‐based intervention (e‐mail, website, computer program) to increase the number of reported infectious diseases (non‐occupational diseases) Wellman 2004 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning carpal tunnel syndrome; study provided information about aetiology Wu 1996 The article described the establishment of a surveillance system concerning occupational diseases, based on hospital centres of consultation. Wu 1996a The article briefly described the establishment of a surveillance system concerning occupational diseases, based on hospital centres of consultation Wu 1998 Descriptive study reporting data of a surveillance system concerning occupational hearing loss; study provided incidence estimates and information about aetiology Open in a new tab Differences between protocol and review The first and the last author exchanged places on the byline. We improved the search strategy by adding the following MeSH Terms: mandatory reporting[MH] and sentinel surveillance[MH]. Contrary to what we had planned, we did not include uncontrolled before‐and‐after studies to provide contrast to the higher quality studies in the discussion. We realised how difficult it would have been to decide what to include in this category when we screened all the results of our systematic searches. Because of the extreme heterogeneity of uncontrolled before‐and‐after studies, it would have been very difficult to make any informative statements about these studies as a whole. To assess the risk of bias of RCTs and CBA studies, we used only the items on internal validity of the checklist proposed by Downs 1998 and not those on reporting quality or external validity. The 13 items of the checklist include the domains of the ''Risk of bias'' tool recommended in Chapter 8 of the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions ( Higgins 2011a ): random sequence generation, allocation concealment, blinding of participants and personnel, blinding of outcome assessment, incomplete outcome data, and selective reporting. We adapted the answers to the questions of the checklist slightly so that they fit the ''Risk of bias'' tool as implemented in RevMan 2014 by using the rating of ''high risk of bias'', ''low risk of bias'', or ''unclear risk of bias'' instead of using scores 0 or 1 as proposed by the checklist authors. Contributions of authors SC and SM conceived and prepared the protocol and review together with RS, DS, AD, MV, and SR. SC co‐ordinated the review process. SC, MV, and SM developed and conducted the search strategy. All authors participated in the screening of eligible studies to include. SC, RS, DS, AD, and SR extracted the data and assessed risk of bias. SC and SM conducted the data synthesis. SC and SM drafted the text. All review authors reviewed and approved the final text. Sources of support Internal sources Department of Medical and Surgical Sciences, University of Bologna, Italy. Salary for Stefania Curti and Stefano Mattioli. Finnish Institute of Occupational Health, Tampere, Finland. Salary for Riitta Sauni. Epidemiology and Social Medicine, University of Antwerp, Antwerpen, Belgium. Salary for Antoon De Schryver. Département Santé Travail, Institut de Veille Sanitaire, Saint Maurice, France. Salary for Madeleine Valenty and Stéphanie Rivière. External sources Cochrane Occupational Safety and Health Review Group, Finland. Provided a bursary stipend to the sum of EUR 5000, minus 35% tax (i.e. EUR 3250), paid in three installments provided that work proceeded according to agreed deadlines. COST Action IS1002 Modernet, European Union, Other. Covered all the costs of arranging meetings between the authors. Declarations of interest Stefania Curti: None known. Riitta Sauni: None known. Dick Spreeuwers: None known. Antoon de Schryver: None known. Madeleine Valenty: None known. Stéphanie Rivière: None known. Stephano Mattioli: None known. New References References to studies included in this review Bailie 1998 {published data only} Bailie R, London L. Enhanced surveillance for pesticide poisoning in the Western Cape‐‐an elusive target. South African Medical Journal. 1998/11/03 1998; Vol. 88, issue 9:1105‐9. [ PubMed ] Brissette 2006a {published data only} Brissette I, Gelberg KH, Grey AJ. The effect of message type on physician compliance with disease reporting requirements. Public Health Reports 2006;121(6):703‐9. [ D