INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3861,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1757593','Development and evaluation of a quality assessment instrument for occupational physicians.','van der Weide WE, Verbeek JH, van Dijk FJ, Hulshof CT.','1998','rehabilitated demarcate deviant deviance deviant deviated deviant agius symington seaton agius agius seaton antti poika kuykendall wun wray bigos battie mcglynn nejm199609263351311 buckle stubbs casparie coste delecoeuillerie parc paolaggi crombie fardy jeffs gerbert goertz cretin worthman dorey soloman kemeny gerbert gullion qrb s0097 klazinga koes tulder windt bouter ajot lawthers fowles garnick dempaq improv s1070 lomas marras fathallah allread rajulu occupationally mcneill sinkora leavitt rothrock nesson durieux bf01705730 schellevis eijk lisdonk velden weel soter austen chemotactic nejm197603252941302 madhok vlaeyen kole snijders boeren eek korff cherkin korff ormel dworkin somerville fabq wensing aaohn kort s28 weide doef weide tulder','Abstract OBJECTIVES: To develop and apply a method for assessing the quality of the process of occupational health care for individual patients. METHODS: The scientific literature was studied to develop a method to assess the quality of the process of occupational rehabilitation for workers with low back pain. The method was applied to health care and university workers with low back pain who were rehabilitated by their occupational physicians. RESULTS: Assessment of quality of care is regarded as a four step approach. Firstly, guidelines should be developed and implemented. Secondly, indicators for quality and criteria to demarcate good and deviant quality were derived from the guidelines. Thirdly, a method for data collection was chosen. Finally, quality was scored. For occupational rehabilitation, there was some deviance from the guidelines for most cases, especially in continuity of care with a deviant rate of 47%. Other indicators deviated from 1.4%- 17.4%. Occupational physicians agreed on the relevance of the indicators and criteria, but for three indicators they evaluated the criteria as too rigid. They did not agree with their own performance scores in 66% of the deviant cases. CONCLUSION: Assessing the quality of the process of occupational health care with this method is an asset to present methods, but more specific criteria are needed for a more sensitive assessment. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (136.8 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Agius R. M., Lee R. J., Symington I. S., Riddle H. F., Seaton A. An audit of occupational medicine consultation records. Occup Med (Lond) 1994 Jul;44(3):151–157. doi: 10.1093/occmed/44.3.151. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Agius R. M. Peer review audit in occupational medicine. J Soc Occup Med. 1990 Autumn;40(3):87–88. doi: 10.1093/occmed/40.3.87. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Agius R. M., Seaton A., Lee R. J. Audit of sickness absence and fitness-for-work referrals. Occup Med (Lond) 1995 Jun;45(3):125–130. doi: 10.1093/occmed/45.3.125. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Antti-Poika M. Implementation of a quality system for the examination of occupational diseases. Occup Med (Lond) 1995 Dec;45(6):298–304. doi: 10.1093/occmed/45.6.298. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ashton C. M., Kuykendall D. H., Johnson M. L., Wun C. C., Wray N. P., Carr M. J., Slater C. H., Wu L., Bush G. R. A method of developing and weighting explicit process of care criteria for quality assessment. Med Care. 1994 Aug;32(8):755–770. doi: 10.1097/00005650-199408000-00001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Berwick D. M. Health services research and quality of care. Assignments for the 1990s. Med Care. 1989 Aug;27(8):763–771. doi: 10.1097/00005650-198908000-00001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bigos S. J., Battié M. C. Acute care to prevent back disability. Ten years of progress. Clin